window.TWG_I18N=window.TWG_I18N||{};TWG_I18N["zh-hant"]=Object.assign(TWG_I18N["zh-hant"]||{},{"Hi! I'm your sake goat 🍶":"嗨！我是你的清酒山羊 🍶","I'll build you a tailor-made path to pass your Sake 1 exam. It takes 5 quick questions.":"我會為你量身打造一條通過清酒 1 考試的學習路線，只要回答 5 個簡單問題。","Sake 1 exam":"清酒 1 考試","Lessons, quizzes and flashcards on rice, kōji, sake grades and service.":"關於米、麴、清酒等級與侍酒服務的課程、測驗和字卡。","Review Japanese terms, grades and styles":"複習日文術語、等級與風格","All of Sake 1":"清酒 1 全部內容","Lessons, flashcards and mistakes: only the Sake 1 syllabus.":"課程、字卡與錯題：只包含清酒 1 的考試大綱。","Sake 1 games":"清酒 1 遊戲","Short games on Sake 1 questions and terms.":"用清酒 1 的題目和術語玩的小遊戲。","What sake is":"清酒是什麼","The brewing steps":"釀造步驟","From brown rice to steamed rice":"從糙米到蒸米","Making kōji":"製麴","Fermentation and style":"發酵與風格","Basic and premium sake":"普通清酒與高級清酒","Reading a Japanese label":"看懂日文酒標","Speciality styles":"特殊風格","Tasting sake":"品飲清酒","Storage and faults":"保存與缺陷","Sake and food":"清酒與餐點","Ingredients and brewing":"原料與釀造","Categories and styles":"分類與風格","k08":"k08","Tasting, storage and service":"品飲、保存與侍酒","k11":"k11","What is sake made from?":"清酒是用什麼做的？","Rice":"米","Malted barley":"大麥麥芽","Sweet potatoes":"地瓜","Sake is a Japanese alcoholic drink made from rice.":"清酒是以米釀造的日本酒精飲料。","Malted barley is the base of beer and grapes of wine. Sweet potatoes are used for other drinks, not sake.":"大麥麥芽是啤酒的原料，葡萄是葡萄酒的原料。地瓜用於其他飲料，不用於清酒。","What does nihon-shu, one of sake's official names, literally mean?":"清酒的正式名稱之一「日本酒」（nihon-shu），字面上是什麼意思？","Japanese alcoholic drink":"日本的酒精飲料","Fine rice wine":"上等米酒","Pure rice":"純粹的米","Big sake":"大的酒","Nihon-shu means 'Japanese alcoholic drink': the name ties sake to its country.":"「日本酒」的意思是「日本的酒精飲料」：這個名稱把清酒和它的國家連在一起。","'Pure rice' is the meaning of junmai, a label term. 'Big' recalls the dai in daiginjō. No official name means 'fine rice wine'.":"「純粹的米」是酒標用語「純米」的意思。「大」讓人想到「大吟釀」的「大」。沒有任何正式名稱是「上等米酒」的意思。","What is the typical alcohol level of a bottled sake?":"瓶裝清酒的酒精濃度通常是多少？","15–17% abv":"15–17% abv","4–6% abv":"4–6% abv","9–11% abv":"9–11% abv","38–40% abv":"38–40% abv","Most sakes are between 15 and 17% abv.":"大多數清酒的酒精濃度在 15% 到 17% abv 之間。","4–6% is the level of many beers and 38–40% that of a spirit. 9–11% is too low for a typical sake, although some sparkling sakes are light.":"4–6% 是許多啤酒的濃度，38–40% 是烈酒的濃度。9–11% 對一般清酒來說太低，不過有些氣泡清酒比較清淡。","Which description fits a typical sake?":"哪一項描述符合典型的清酒？","Clear and colourless, slightly sweet, lightly acidic":"清澈無色、微甜、酸度輕","Amber, very bitter, fizzy":"琥珀色、非常苦、有氣泡","Cloudy, dry, very tannic":"混濁、不甜、單寧很重","Ruby red, tart, fruity":"寶石紅、酸、果香","Typical sake is clear and colourless, slightly sweet and lightly acidic, with delicate flavours.":"典型的清酒清澈無色、微甜、酸度輕，風味細緻。","Cloudy describes nigori and amber is often koshu: these are speciality styles, not typical sake. Sake is never red or tannic, and marked bitterness belongs to beer rather than sake.":"混濁是濁酒的特徵，琥珀色常見於古酒：這些都是特殊風格，不是典型清酒。清酒從來不是紅色，也沒有單寧；明顯的苦味屬於啤酒而不是清酒。","Which of these is NOT one of the four main ingredients of sake?":"下列哪一項不是清酒的四種主要原料之一？","High-strength distilled alcohol":"高濃度蒸餾酒精","Distilled alcohol is an optional extra, not one of the four main ingredients.":"蒸餾酒精是可選的添加物，不是四種主要原料之一。","Steamed white rice, kōji, water and yeast are in every sake. Distilled alcohol is added only in some cases, before filtration.":"蒸過的白米、麴、水和酵母存在於每一款清酒中。蒸餾酒精只有在部分情況下、於過濾前添加。","In what state does rice usually arrive at a sake brewery?":"米送到清酒酒藏時通常是什麼狀態？","Raw, hard and dusty":"生的、堅硬、帶粉塵","Already steamed":"已經蒸好","Already turned into kōji":"已經做成麴","Ground into flour":"磨成米粉","Rice is bought raw; steaming is done in the brewery.":"米是以生米購入的，蒸米在酒藏裡進行。","Kōji is made in the brewery from steamed rice. Rice is not ground into flour: it is polished, washed, soaked and steamed.":"麴是在酒藏裡用蒸米製作的。米不會被磨成粉，而是經過精米、洗米、浸泡和蒸米。","Why is the rice steamed?":"為什麼要蒸米？","To soften it so it can break up in the water":"讓米變軟，才能在水中分解","To turn its starch into alcohol":"把澱粉轉化成酒精","To give it an amber colour":"讓米呈現琥珀色","To remove the outer layers":"去除外層","Steam softens the hard rice, which can then break up in the water during fermentation.":"蒸氣讓堅硬的米變軟，之後在發酵過程中就能在水中分解。","The outer layers are removed by polishing, not steaming. Starch becomes sugar thanks to kōji enzymes, then alcohol thanks to yeast. Steaming does not colour the rice.":"外層是靠精米去除的，不是靠蒸。澱粉靠麴的酵素變成糖，再靠酵母變成酒精。蒸米不會讓米變色。","What is kōji?":"麴是什麼？","Steamed rice on which a special mould has been grown":"長了特殊黴菌的蒸米","A selected yeast bought from a supplier":"向供應商購買的精選酵母","A neutral alcohol added before filtration":"過濾前添加的中性酒精","The rice dust removed during polishing":"精米時去除的米糠粉","Kōji is steamed rice covered in a special mould that produces enzymes.":"麴是覆滿特殊黴菌的蒸米，這種黴菌會產生酵素。","Yeast is a separate ingredient that turns sugar into alcohol. Neutral alcohol is the optional ingredient. Polishing dust is washed off and not used.":"酵母是另一種原料，負責把糖變成酒精。中性酒精是可選原料。精米的粉末會被洗掉，不會使用。","How much of the rice is used to make kōji?":"做麴會用掉多少米？","A small proportion of the steamed rice":"一小部分的蒸米","All of the steamed rice":"全部的蒸米","Only the brown outer layers":"只用褐色的外層","Unpolished raw rice":"未精米的生米","Only a small proportion of the steamed rice is taken to grow the mould on.":"只取一小部分蒸米來培養黴菌。","The rest of the steamed rice is the starch source in fermentation. Kōji is made from polished, steamed rice, not raw rice or the outer layers.":"其餘的蒸米是發酵時的澱粉來源。麴是用精米後蒸過的米做的，不是用生米或外層。","What water do sake breweries usually use?":"清酒酒藏通常使用什麼水？","Local water: almost any, as long as it is clean":"當地的水：幾乎任何水都可以，只要乾淨","Only desalinated seawater":"只用淡化海水","Only factory-bought distilled water":"只用工廠購買的蒸餾水","Water imported from a single Japanese spring":"只用從單一日本泉源進口的水","Local supplies are used; almost any water works if it is clean.":"使用當地的水源；幾乎任何乾淨的水都可以。","There is no rule requiring distilled water, seawater or a single spring. The simple rule to remember: local, clean water.":"沒有規定一定要用蒸餾水、海水或單一泉源。要記住的簡單原則：當地、乾淨的水。","Where do the yeasts used for sake usually come from?":"清酒用的酵母通常從哪裡來？","From specialist suppliers selling specific varieties":"來自專門供應商販售的特定品種","From the kōji mould":"來自麴菌","From the husk of brown rice":"來自糙米的外殼","From the added distilled alcohol":"來自添加的蒸餾酒精","Breweries buy specific yeast varieties from specialist suppliers.":"酒藏向專門供應商購買特定的酵母品種。","The kōji mould produces enzymes; it is not the yeast. Distilled alcohol contains no live yeast, and yeasts are not taken from the rice.":"麴菌產生的是酵素，它不是酵母。蒸餾酒精裡沒有活酵母，酵母也不是從米裡取得的。","During alcoholic fermentation, yeast turns sugar into…":"酒精發酵時，酵母把糖轉化成…","alcohol and carbon dioxide":"酒精和二氧化碳","starch and water":"澱粉和水","enzymes and oxygen":"酵素和氧氣","kōji and alcohol":"麴和酒精","Yeast eats sugar and makes alcohol, releasing carbon dioxide.":"酵母吃掉糖、產生酒精，同時釋放二氧化碳。","Starch is the starting point, not the product. Enzymes come from kōji, which is made before fermentation: yeast does not make them from sugar.":"澱粉是起點，不是產物。酵素來自麴，而麴是在發酵前做好的：酵母不會用糖製造酵素。","Why could yeast alone not turn rice into alcohol?":"為什麼光靠酵母無法把米變成酒精？","Because rice contains starch and yeast cannot eat starch":"因為米含有澱粉，而酵母不能吃澱粉","Because rice contains too much sugar":"因為米含糖太多","Because yeast needs grapes":"因為酵母需要葡萄","Because rice is too acidic":"因為米太酸","Rice has no sugar, only starch: without kōji enzymes the yeast has nothing to eat.":"米裡沒有糖，只有澱粉：沒有麴的酵素，酵母就沒有東西可吃。","Rice contains no sugar, so it cannot have too much. Yeast does not need grapes: sugar dissolved in water is enough. Acidity is not the issue.":"米不含糖，所以不可能含糖太多。酵母不需要葡萄：溶在水裡的糖就夠了。酸度也不是問題。","What do enzymes do during starch conversion?":"澱粉轉化時，酵素的作用是什麼？","They break starch chains into individual sugars":"把澱粉鏈切成一個個的糖","They turn alcohol back into sugar":"把酒精變回糖","They join sugars together into starch":"把糖連結成澱粉","They remove the carbon dioxide":"去除二氧化碳","Enzymes unlock the bonds between the sugar molecules in starch, freeing sugars the yeast can eat.":"酵素解開澱粉中糖分子之間的鍵結，釋放出酵母能吃的糖。","Joining sugars into starch is the opposite of what is needed. Enzymes do not act on alcohol or carbon dioxide, which are made by the yeast.":"把糖連結成澱粉正好與需要的相反。酵素不作用於酒精或二氧化碳，這兩者是酵母產生的。","Where do the enzymes that turn rice starch into sugar come from?":"把米的澱粉轉化成糖的酵素從哪裡來？","From the mould grown to make kōji":"來自為了製麴而培養的黴菌","From the yeast":"來自酵母","From the spring water":"來自泉水","From the distilled alcohol":"來自蒸餾酒精","The kōji mould produces the enzymes: that is why kōji is essential.":"麴菌產生酵素：這就是麴不可或缺的原因。","Yeast turns sugar into alcohol but cannot break down starch. Water and distilled alcohol bring no enzymes for conversion.":"酵母能把糖變成酒精，但不能分解澱粉。水和蒸餾酒精都不帶來轉化所需的酵素。","Which three steps ALWAYS happen when sake is made?":"釀造清酒時，哪三個步驟一定會發生？","Fermentation, filtration, bottling":"發酵、過濾、裝瓶","Fermentation, pasteurisation, adding water":"發酵、加熱殺菌、加水","Adding alcohol, filtration, ageing":"添加酒精、過濾、熟成","Distillation, filtration, bottling":"蒸餾、過濾、裝瓶","Fermentation, filtration and bottling happen for every sake.":"每一款清酒都會經過發酵、過濾和裝瓶。","Pasteurisation, adding water and adding alcohol are optional. Sake is not distilled, and ageing applies only to one speciality style, koshu.":"加熱殺菌、加水和添加酒精都是可選的。清酒不經蒸餾，而熟成只適用於一種特殊風格：古酒。","Which of these steps is OPTIONAL?":"下列哪個步驟是可選的？","Pasteurisation":"加熱殺菌","Bottling":"裝瓶","Pasteurisation is very common but not compulsory: sakes that skip it are called nama.":"加熱殺菌（火入）非常普遍，但不是強制的：省略這一步的清酒稱為「生酒」。","Filtration, fermentation and bottling always happen; filtration is even required by law.":"過濾、發酵和裝瓶一定會發生；過濾甚至是法律規定的。","What is the fermentation starter for?":"酒母的作用是什麼？","To build a healthy, active yeast population":"培養出健康、活躍的酵母群","To remove the rice solids":"去除米的固形物","To lower the alcohol level":"降低酒精濃度","To pasteurise the sake":"為清酒加熱殺菌","A little of the ingredients is used to multiply the yeast; the starter then goes into the main fermentation.":"用少量原料讓酵母大量繁殖，之後酒母再加入主發酵。","Solids are removed by filtration, alcohol is lowered with water, and pasteurisation is a heat treatment: all later steps.":"固形物靠過濾去除，酒精濃度靠加水降低，加熱殺菌則是熱處理：這些都是之後的步驟。","What is meant by 'parallel fermentation' in sake?":"清酒的「並行發酵」是什麼意思？","Enzymes make sugar while yeast turns it into alcohol":"酵素製造糖的同時，酵母把糖變成酒精","Two tanks ferment the same sake side by side":"兩個槽並排發酵同一款清酒","Rice and grapes are fermented together":"米和葡萄一起發酵","The sake ferments a second time in the bottle":"清酒在瓶中進行第二次發酵","Starch conversion and alcoholic fermentation happen at the same time: this is unique to sake.":"澱粉轉化和酒精發酵同時進行：這是清酒獨有的。","'Parallel' refers to the two simultaneous reactions, not two tanks. There are no grapes in sake, and a second fermentation in bottle is not what the term means.":"「並行」指的是兩個同時發生的反應，不是兩個槽。清酒裡沒有葡萄，瓶中二次發酵也不是這個詞的意思。","How much alcohol does sake usually have at the end of fermentation?":"發酵結束時，清酒通常有多少酒精？","About 20% abv":"約 20% abv","About 5% abv":"約 5% abv","About 40% abv":"約 40% abv","At the end of fermentation sake is usually around 20%; water then brings it down to 15–17%.":"發酵結束時清酒通常約為 20%；之後加水降到 15–17%。","5% is beer strength and 12% light-wine strength. 40% is spirit strength, impossible to reach by fermentation alone.":"5% 是啤酒的濃度，12% 是清淡葡萄酒的濃度。40% 是烈酒的濃度，光靠發酵不可能達到。","Why is the fermenting liquid white and cloudy?":"為什麼發酵中的液體是白色而混濁的？","Because of the rice in the tank":"因為槽裡有米","Because of the added distilled alcohol":"因為添加了蒸餾酒精","Because milk is added to the recipe":"因為配方裡加了牛奶","Because of pasteurisation":"因為加熱殺菌","The white colour comes from the rice: the solids are removed later by filtration.":"白色來自米：固形物之後會靠過濾去除。","Neutral alcohol is clear. No milk is added to sake: 'lactic' aromas come from fermentation. Pasteurisation happens later, on filtered sake.":"中性酒精是透明的。清酒不加牛奶：「乳酸」香氣來自發酵。加熱殺菌在之後才進行，處理的是過濾後的清酒。","What does the law say about filtering sake?":"關於清酒過濾，法律怎麼規定？","Every sake must be filtered":"每一款清酒都必須過濾","Filtration is banned for premium sakes":"高級清酒禁止過濾","Only export sake is filtered":"只有外銷的清酒會過濾","The law says nothing about it":"法律沒有相關規定","By law, all sake must be filtered after fermentation.":"依法，所有清酒在發酵後都必須過濾。","Even cloudy nigori is filtered, just roughly. No rule exempts premium sake or applies only to exports.":"就連混濁的濁酒也有過濾，只是過濾得比較粗。沒有任何規定讓高級清酒免除過濾，也沒有只適用於外銷的規定。","What does filtration remove?":"過濾去除的是什麼？","The rice solids":"米的固形物","Excess alcohol":"多餘的酒精","The carbon dioxide":"二氧化碳","The sweetness":"甜味","Filtering separates out the rice solids and usually leaves a clear liquid.":"過濾會分離出米的固形物，通常留下清澈的液體。","Alcohol is lowered by adding water, not by filtering. Filtration removes neither gas nor sweetness.":"酒精濃度是靠加水降低，不是靠過濾。過濾既不去除氣體，也不去除甜味。","What container is nearly all sake packed in?":"幾乎所有清酒都用什麼容器包裝？","Glass bottles":"玻璃瓶","Cans":"罐","Wooden casks":"木桶","Cartons":"紙盒","Nearly all sake goes into glass bottles of various sizes.":"幾乎所有清酒都裝在各種容量的玻璃瓶裡。","Cans and cartons exist but are rare. Wood, and oak especially, is very rarely used, even for ageing.":"罐裝和紙盒裝雖然存在但很少見。木材，尤其是橡木，很少使用，連熟成也很少用。","Why does a brewer add neutral distilled alcohol before filtration?":"為什麼釀酒師會在過濾前添加中性蒸餾酒精？","To extract flavours trapped in the rice solids":"萃取困在米固形物中的風味","To turn the sake into a spirit":"把清酒變成烈酒","To restart fermentation":"重新啟動發酵","To give the sake colour":"為清酒上色","The neutral alcohol helps draw out flavours left in the solids.":"中性酒精有助於萃取殘留在固形物中的風味。","Sake stays a fermented drink and is later diluted with water. The alcohol does not restart fermentation and adds no colour: it is neutral.":"清酒仍然是發酵酒，之後會加水稀釋。酒精不會重新啟動發酵，也不會帶來顏色：它是中性的。","At what point is distilled alcohol added, when it is used?":"如果使用蒸餾酒精，是在什麼時候添加？","Before filtration":"過濾前","Before steaming the rice":"蒸米前","After bottling":"裝瓶後","While making kōji":"製麴時","It is added at the end of fermentation, before filtering, while the rice solids are still present.":"在發酵結束、過濾之前添加，此時米的固形物仍然存在。","Its job is to extract flavour from the solids: after filtration or bottling there are none left. It has no role in kōji or steaming.":"它的作用是從固形物中萃取風味：過濾或裝瓶後就沒有固形物了。它與製麴或蒸米無關。","Why is water nearly always added to sake before bottling?":"為什麼清酒在裝瓶前幾乎都會加水？","To lower the alcohol from about 20% to 15–17%":"把酒精從約 20% 降到 15–17%","To make it cloudy":"讓它變混濁","To pasteurise it":"為它加熱殺菌","To make it sparkling":"讓它產生氣泡","Water takes sake from its end-of-fermentation strength (about 20%) to the usual 15–17%.":"加水讓清酒從發酵結束時的濃度（約 20%）降到一般的 15–17%。","Cloudiness comes from rice solids left in nigori, bubbles from carbon dioxide and pasteurisation from heat: water does none of these.":"混濁來自濁酒中保留的米固形物，氣泡來自二氧化碳，加熱殺菌靠的是熱：加水不會造成這些。","What is pasteurisation of sake?":"清酒的加熱殺菌是什麼？","A heat treatment before the sake goes on sale":"清酒上市前的熱處理","Ageing in cedar casks":"在杉木桶中熟成","An addition of sugar":"添加糖","A second filtration through charcoal":"以木炭進行第二次過濾","Pasteurising means heating the sake before it is sold.":"加熱殺菌就是在清酒販售前將它加熱。","Pasteurisation has nothing to do with wood, sugar or charcoal: it is purely about heat.":"加熱殺菌與木材、糖或木炭無關：純粹是加熱。","What does pasteurisation achieve?":"加熱殺菌能達到什麼效果？","It prevents faulty aromas from developing":"防止產生缺陷香氣","It raises the alcohol level":"提高酒精濃度","It adds fruity and floral aromas":"增加果香和花香","It makes the sake cloudy":"讓清酒變混濁","Heat stabilises the sake and stops faulty aromas from appearing.":"加熱讓清酒穩定，並防止缺陷香氣出現。","Fruity, floral aromas come from highly polished rice, special yeasts and cool fermentation. Pasteurisation changes neither strength nor clarity.":"果香和花香來自高度精米的米、特殊酵母和低溫發酵。加熱殺菌既不改變酒精濃度，也不改變清澈度。","A sake has just finished fermenting: which order of the next steps is correct?":"一款清酒剛完成發酵：接下來步驟的哪個順序正確？","Optional alcohol → filtration → water → bottling":"可選的酒精 → 過濾 → 水 → 裝瓶","Filtration → alcohol → bottling → water":"過濾 → 酒精 → 裝瓶 → 水","Bottling → filtration → alcohol → water":"裝瓶 → 過濾 → 酒精 → 水","Water → bottling → alcohol → filtration":"水 → 裝瓶 → 酒精 → 過濾","Alcohol comes before filtration (it needs the solids) and water before bottling.":"酒精在過濾之前（它需要固形物），水在裝瓶之前。","Adding alcohol after filtration would be pointless: there would be no solids left to extract flavour from. Water adjusts the strength before the sake is bottled.":"過濾後才加酒精沒有意義：已經沒有固形物可以萃取風味了。水是在清酒裝瓶前用來調整濃度的。","Which four stages turn brown rice into steamed white rice?":"哪四個階段把糙米變成蒸好的白米？","Polishing, washing, soaking, steaming":"精米、洗米、浸泡、蒸米","Malting, milling, mashing, boiling":"製麥、碾磨、糖化、煮沸","Washing, drying, roasting, grinding":"清洗、乾燥、烘烤、研磨","Soaking, germination, drying, polishing":"浸泡、發芽、乾燥、精米","Brown rice is polished, washed, soaked and steamed.":"糙米要經過精米、洗米、浸泡和蒸米。","Malting, milling and mashing belong to beer. Sake rice is neither germinated nor roasted.":"製麥、碾磨和糖化屬於啤酒。清酒用米既不發芽也不烘烤。","How is rice polished?":"米是怎麼精米的？","Grains pass between two rough surfaces for a few hours":"米粒在兩個粗糙表面之間通過數小時","Grains are dissolved in boiling water":"把米粒溶在滾水中","A mould eats away the outer layers":"讓黴菌吃掉外層","Grains are roasted until the husk falls off":"烘烤米粒直到外殼脫落","Two rough surfaces slowly wear away the outer and some middle layers over a few hours.":"兩個粗糙表面在數小時內慢慢磨掉外層和部分中層。","The mould comes later, on steamed rice, to make kōji. The rice is neither dissolved nor roasted.":"黴菌是之後才在蒸米上培養，用來做麴。米既不會被溶解也不會被烘烤。","What colour are the middle layers of a rice grain?":"米粒的中層是什麼顏色？","Green":"綠色","The outer layers are brown; the middle layers and core are white.":"外層是褐色；中層和米芯是白色。","Brown is the colour of the outer layers, which are nearly always removed. Neither green nor black describes any part of the grain.":"褐色是外層的顏色，而外層幾乎都會被去除。綠色和黑色都不是米粒任何部分的顏色。","What is the core of a rice grain made of, almost entirely?":"米粒的米芯幾乎完全由什麼構成？","Starch":"澱粉","Fats and proteins":"脂肪和蛋白質","The core is almost pure starch: that is why heavy polishing gives purer sakes.":"米芯幾乎是純澱粉：這就是高度精米能釀出更純淨清酒的原因。","Rice contains no sugar, and the 'other components' that give acidity and umami sit mainly in the outer and middle layers.":"米不含糖，而帶來酸度和鮮味的「其他成分」主要位於外層和中層。","What do the outer and middle layers of the grain bring to sake?":"米粒的外層和中層為清酒帶來什麼？","More acidity and umami, with cereal and lactic aromas":"更多酸度和鮮味，以及穀物和乳酸香氣","Pure fruity and floral aromas":"純淨的果香和花香","Natural bubbles":"天然氣泡","An amber colour and dried-fruit aromas":"琥珀色和果乾香氣","Besides starch they contain other components that give acidity, umami and cereal, lactic notes.":"除了澱粉，它們還含有其他成分，帶來酸度、鮮味以及穀物、乳酸調性。","Fruit and flowers come when these layers are almost entirely removed. Amber and dried fruit belong to aged koshu; bubbles come from carbon dioxide.":"果香和花香要在這些層幾乎完全去除後才會出現。琥珀色和果乾屬於熟成的古酒；氣泡來自二氧化碳。","A brewer removes almost all the middle layers, leaving mainly the core. What style will usually result?":"釀酒師去除了幾乎所有中層，主要留下米芯。通常會得到什麼風格？","Less acidity and umami, more fruity and floral aromas":"酸度和鮮味較少，果香和花香較多","More acidity and umami, cereal aromas":"酸度和鮮味較多，穀物香氣","A cloudy sake with rice fragments":"帶有米粒碎片的混濁清酒","A stronger sake, above 25% abv":"更烈的清酒，超過 25% abv","Keeping almost pure starch removes the components behind acidity and umami, so fruit and flowers take over.":"留下幾乎純粹的澱粉，就去除了造成酸度和鮮味的成分，於是果香和花香占上風。","More acidity and cereal notes come from light polishing. Cloudiness depends on rough filtration, not polishing, which does not change the final strength either.":"較多的酸度和穀物調性來自輕度精米。混濁取決於粗過濾，而不是精米；精米也不會改變最終的酒精濃度。","Who sets the minimum polishing for the premium grades?":"誰規定高級等級的最低精米程度？","Japanese law":"日本法律","Each brewery, freely":"各酒藏自由決定","The rice suppliers":"米的供應商","The importers in each country":"各國的進口商","The polishing ratio required for each premium grade is defined in Japanese law.":"每個高級等級所要求的精米步合由日本法律規定。","Precisely because it is law rather than free choice, the label terms are a reliable guide to style.":"正因為這是法律而不是自由選擇，酒標用語才是判斷風格的可靠依據。","A label says 'polishing ratio 60%'. What does this mean?":"酒標寫著「精米步合 60%」。這是什麼意思？","60% of the grain remains: 40% has been removed":"米粒保留 60%：去除了 40%","60% of the grain has been removed":"米粒去除了 60%","The sake contains 60% rice and 40% water":"清酒含 60% 的米和 40% 的水","60% of the rice became kōji":"60% 的米做成了麴","The polishing ratio shows the part of the grain left after polishing.":"精米步合表示精米後留下的米粒比例。","This is the most common mistake: the number shows what remains, not what is removed. It has nothing to do with water or the amount of kōji.":"這是最常見的錯誤：數字表示留下的部分，不是去除的部分。它與水或麴的分量無關。","Which rice has been polished the MOST?":"哪一種米精米程度最高？","Polishing ratio 45%":"精米步合 45%","Polishing ratio 60%":"精米步合 60%","Polishing ratio 70%":"精米步合 70%","Polishing ratio 90%":"精米步合 90%","At 45% the least grain remains: this is the most polished rice.":"45% 留下的米粒最少：這是精米程度最高的米。","The higher the number, the more grain remains and the less polishing: 90% is the least polished of the four.":"數字越高，留下的米粒越多、精米越少：90% 是四者中精米程度最低的。","What is the maximum polishing ratio allowed for daiginjō and junmai daiginjō?":"大吟釀和純米大吟釀允許的最高精米步合是多少？","For daiginjō, 50% or less of the grain must remain.":"大吟釀必須保留 50% 以下的米粒。","60% is the limit for ginjō and junmai ginjō, 70% for honjōzō and most junmai. 80% matches no premium grade.":"60% 是吟釀和純米吟釀的上限，70% 是本釀造和大多數純米的上限。80% 不符合任何高級等級。","Which grade requires a polishing ratio of 60% or less?":"哪個等級要求精米步合 60% 以下？","Ginjō and junmai ginjō: 60% or less.":"吟釀和純米吟釀：60% 以下。","Honjōzō stops at 70% and junmai daiginjō goes down to 50%. Futsū-shu is basic sake with no premium threshold.":"本釀造止於 70%，純米大吟釀則降到 50%。普通酒是基本清酒，沒有高級等級的門檻。","What is true of the polishing ratio for junmai?":"關於純米的精米步合，哪一項正確？","Usually 70%; rice up to 100% may be used, but this is very rare":"通常為 70%；可以使用高達 100% 的米，但非常少見","It must always be 50% or less":"一定要 50% 以下","It may never go below 70%":"永遠不能低於 70%","Junmai does not use polished rice":"純米不用精米過的米","Junmai is usually made with rice polished to 70%; 100–70% is allowed but very rare.":"純米通常使用精米到 70% 的米；100–70% 也被允許，但非常少見。","50% applies to daiginjō. A junmai may be polished further than 70% (junmai ginjō is also junmai), so there is no ban on going lower.":"50% 適用於大吟釀。純米可以精米到低於 70%（純米吟釀也是純米），所以並沒有禁止降得更低。","Why must rice be washed after polishing?":"為什麼精米後必須洗米？","To remove the dust from the layers just polished away":"去除剛磨掉的米層留下的粉末","To give the rice a floral aroma":"讓米帶有花香","To start fermentation":"啟動發酵","To remove the starch from the core":"去除米芯的澱粉","The dust is made of the outer and middle layers the brewer wanted to remove.":"這些粉末來自釀酒師想要去除的外層和中層。","The starch in the core is exactly what is needed to make alcohol, so it stays. Washing adds no aroma and does not start fermentation.":"米芯的澱粉正是製造酒精所需要的，所以會保留。洗米不會增加香氣，也不會啟動發酵。","What are soaking and steaming for?":"浸泡和蒸米的作用是什麼？","To give the grain the right texture and moisture to break up during fermentation":"讓米粒有適當的質地和水分，以便在發酵中分解","To polish the grain more deeply":"把米粒磨得更深","To turn the starch into sugar":"把澱粉變成糖","To sterilise the finished sake":"為完成的清酒殺菌","Soaking and steaming soften the grain and give it the right moisture.":"浸泡和蒸米讓米粒變軟，並具有適當的水分。","Starch becomes sugar thanks to kōji enzymes. Polishing comes first, and pasteurising the finished sake is a different step.":"澱粉靠麴的酵素變成糖。精米在前面就已完成，而為完成的清酒加熱殺菌是另一個步驟。","What does the kōji room allow the brewer to control?":"麴室讓釀酒師能控制什麼？","Temperature and humidity, very precisely":"非常精確地控制溫度和濕度","Carbon dioxide pressure":"二氧化碳壓力","The alcohol level of the sake":"清酒的酒精濃度","Light and noise":"光線和噪音","Kōji rooms let the brewer fine-tune warmth and humidity, which drive mould growth.":"麴室讓釀酒師能細微調整溫度和濕度，而這兩者決定黴菌的生長。","There is no alcohol or fermentation gas yet in kōji making: it is just about growing a mould under the right conditions.":"製麴時還沒有酒精或發酵氣體：重點只是在適當條件下培養黴菌。","What is the FIRST stage of making kōji?":"製麴的第一個階段是什麼？","Cooling the steamed rice":"讓蒸米冷卻","Spreading the mould spores":"撒上黴菌孢子","Turning the rice by hand":"用手翻動米","Moving the rice to the coolest zone":"把米移到最涼的區域","Fresh from the steamer the rice is too hot: it must be cooled first.":"剛出蒸籠的米太燙：必須先冷卻。","Spreading spores is stage two, turning the rice belongs to stage three, and moving it to the coolest zone stops growth at the end.":"撒孢子是第二階段，翻動米屬於第三階段，移到最涼的區域則是在最後停止生長。","How is the rice for kōji cooled?":"做麴用的米怎麼冷卻？","Spread on mats, it cools naturally":"攤在蓆子上自然冷卻","Plunged into iced water":"浸入冰水","Put in a freezer":"放進冷凍庫","Mixed with distilled alcohol":"與蒸餾酒精混合","The rice is spread on mats and left to cool by itself.":"米攤在蓆子上，讓它自然冷卻。","Iced water would make it too wet and a freezer too cold for the mould. Distilled alcohol has nothing to do with kōji.":"冰水會讓米太濕，冷凍庫對黴菌來說太冷。蒸餾酒精與製麴無關。","In which part of the room are the mould spores spread?":"黴菌孢子是撒在麴室的哪個區域？","The warmest, most humid part":"最溫暖、最潮濕的區域","The coolest, driest part":"最涼、最乾燥的區域","Outdoors, in the sun":"戶外，陽光下","In the fermentation tank":"發酵槽裡","Warmth and humidity get the mould growing quickly.":"溫暖和潮濕讓黴菌快速生長。","The coolest zone is used at the end, to stop growth. Kōji is made in its own rooms, not outdoors or in the tank.":"最涼的區域是在最後用來停止生長的。麴是在專用的麴室裡做的，不是在戶外或發酵槽裡。","Why is the rice turned regularly by hand during the initial mould growth?":"為什麼在黴菌初期生長時要定期用手翻動米？","So that the mould grows evenly":"讓黴菌均勻生長","To remove the mould":"去除黴菌","To cool it below freezing":"讓米冷卻到冰點以下","To add the yeast":"加入酵母","Turning spreads warmth and moisture so the growth is even.":"翻動能分散熱度和水分，讓生長均勻。","The mould is wanted, not removed. Yeast comes in at fermentation, and kōji is never taken below freezing.":"黴菌是需要的，不會被去除。酵母在發酵時才加入，麴也從不會降到冰點以下。","How is mould growth slowed and then stopped?":"如何減緩並停止黴菌生長？","By moving the rice to cooler zones, finally the coolest":"把米移到較涼的區域，最後移到最涼的區域","By adding lots of hot water":"加入大量熱水","By steaming the rice again":"再次蒸米","By putting the rice in the dark":"把米放在暗處","Less warmth and humidity slow the mould; the coolest zone stops it.":"溫度和濕度降低會減緩黴菌生長；最涼的區域讓它停止。","Hot water and steam would add warmth and moisture, the opposite of what is needed. Light is not the lever: temperature and humidity are.":"熱水和蒸氣會增加溫度和水分，與需要的正好相反。光線不是關鍵：溫度和濕度才是。","Why does the brewer slow the mould instead of letting it grow freely?":"為什麼釀酒師要減緩黴菌，而不是讓它自由生長？","To get exactly the right amount of mould":"為了得到剛好適量的黴菌","Because mould is a fault":"因為黴菌是一種缺陷","To make the sake sparkling":"讓清酒產生氣泡","To raise the polishing ratio":"提高精米步合","The goal is measured growth: neither too much nor too little mould.":"目標是有節制的生長：黴菌既不能太多也不能太少。","The kōji mould is essential, not a fault. Bubbles and the polishing ratio do not depend on this stage.":"麴菌不可或缺，不是缺陷。氣泡和精米步合都不取決於這個階段。","In nearly all sake styles, which ingredient contributes most to the aromas?":"在幾乎所有清酒風格中，哪種原料對香氣的貢獻最大？","Kōji":"麴","Distilled alcohol":"蒸餾酒精","Yeast contributes a large share of the aromas in nearly all styles.":"在幾乎所有風格中，酵母貢獻了大部分的香氣。","In many styles rice, kōji and water give relatively little flavour. Added alcohol is neutral: it helps extract flavour but brings none of its own.":"在許多風格中，米、麴和水帶來的風味相對較少。添加的酒精是中性的：它有助於萃取風味，但本身不帶風味。","What is needed for the fruity, floral aromas typical of ginjō styles?":"吟釀風格典型的果香和花香需要什麼？","Special yeast strains and lower fermentation temperatures":"特殊酵母菌株和較低的發酵溫度","Any yeast and higher temperatures":"任何酵母和較高的溫度","Lots of water and a long pasteurisation":"大量的水和長時間的加熱殺菌","Ageing in oak":"橡木桶熟成","Ginjō aromas require special yeast strains and a cool fermentation.":"吟釀香氣需要特殊酵母菌株和低溫發酵。","Warm temperatures give cereal and lactic aromas. Water and pasteurisation do not create fruity scents, and oak is very rarely used in sake.":"溫度高會帶來穀物和乳酸香氣。水和加熱殺菌不會產生果香，而清酒很少使用橡木。","What aromas does a warmer fermentation tend to give?":"較溫暖的發酵通常帶來什麼香氣？","Cereal and lactic":"穀物和乳酸","White flowers and delicate fruit":"白花和細緻的水果","Dried fruit and meat":"果乾和肉","Caramel and toffee":"焦糖和太妃糖","More warmth in fermentation = cereal and lactic aromas.":"發酵溫度越高 = 穀物和乳酸香氣。","Flowers and delicate fruit come from cool fermentation. Dried fruit and meat belong to koshu; caramel and toffee often signal an oxidised or old sake.":"花香和細緻的果香來自低溫發酵。果乾和肉屬於古酒；焦糖和太妃糖常代表清酒氧化或過老。","Which combination usually produces a fruity, floral sake with little acidity and umami?":"哪種組合通常會釀出果香、花香明顯，酸度和鮮味少的清酒？","Highly polished rice, special yeasts, cool fermentation":"高度精米、特殊酵母、低溫發酵","Lightly polished rice, warm fermentation":"輕度精米、溫暖發酵","Lightly polished rice, special yeasts, ageing":"輕度精米、特殊酵母、熟成","Highly polished rice, warm fermentation, no filtration":"高度精米、溫暖發酵、不過濾","These are the three factors of the ginjō style: heavy polishing, special yeasts, low temperature.":"這是吟釀風格的三個要素：高度精米、特殊酵母、低溫。","Lightly polished rice and warm fermentation give cereal, lactic notes with more acidity and umami. Filtration is required by law anyway.":"輕度精米和溫暖發酵帶來穀物、乳酸調性，以及更多酸度和鮮味。何況過濾本來就是法律規定的。","During fermentation, which two levers does the brewer use to steer style?":"發酵期間，釀酒師用哪兩個手段來引導風格？","Choice of yeast and temperature":"酵母的選擇和溫度","Choice of bottle and light":"酒瓶的選擇和光線","Type of wood and time in cask":"木材種類和在桶中的時間","Amount of added sugar and gas":"添加的糖量和氣體","Yeast and fermentation temperature change the aroma profile a great deal.":"酵母和發酵溫度會大幅改變香氣表現。","Bottle and light concern storage, not fermentation. Wood is rare in sake, and sugar is not added to steer the style.":"酒瓶和光線關係到保存，不是發酵。清酒很少用木材，也不會加糖來引導風格。","What is basic sake called in Japanese?":"基本清酒的日文名稱是什麼？","Futsū-shu is the name for simple, basic sake.":"普通酒（futsū-shu）是簡單、基本清酒的名稱。","Junmai and daiginjō are premium grades; koshu is the speciality style for aged sake.":"純米和大吟釀是高級等級；古酒是熟成清酒的特殊風格。","What is the main division of sake categories?":"清酒分類的主要劃分是什麼？","Basic sake and premium sake":"普通清酒和高級清酒","White sake and red sake":"白清酒和紅清酒","Dry sake and sweet sake":"不甜清酒和甜清酒","Japanese and foreign sake":"日本清酒和外國清酒","First comes basic (futsū-shu) versus premium, then premium splits into grades.":"首先分為普通（普通酒）與高級，接著高級清酒再分成不同等級。","Sake is not red. Sweetness and origin are not the main basis of the categories.":"清酒沒有紅色的。甜度和產地都不是分類的主要依據。","What do nearly all premium sakes share with basic sake?":"幾乎所有高級清酒與普通清酒有什麼共同點？","The same alcohol level and colour":"相同的酒精濃度和顏色","The same polishing ratio":"相同的精米步合","The same fruity aromas":"相同的果香","The same price":"相同的價格","Most premium sakes have similar strength and colour to futsū-shu; aromas and style change.":"大多數高級清酒的濃度和顏色與普通酒相近；改變的是香氣和風格。","Polishing is exactly what sets the premium grades apart. Aromas differ between the two style families.":"精米正是讓高級等級與眾不同的地方。兩大風格家族的香氣也不同。","Which label word indicates the style with pure floral and fruity aromas?":"哪個酒標用語代表帶有純淨花香和果香的風格？","Ginjō is the key word for the fruity, floral style.":"吟釀是果香、花香風格的關鍵詞。","Honjōzō belongs to the cereal, lactic style. Nama only means unpasteurised, and futsū-shu is basic sake.":"本釀造屬於穀物、乳酸風格。生酒只表示未經加熱殺菌，普通酒則是基本清酒。","What does daiginjō literally mean?":"「大吟釀」字面上是什麼意思？","Big ginjō":"大的吟釀","Aged sake":"熟成的清酒","Raw sake":"生的清酒","Dai means 'big': daiginjō is the 'big ginjō', with the purest aromas.":"「大」是「大」的意思：大吟釀就是「大的吟釀」，香氣最純淨。","'Pure rice' is junmai and 'aged sake' is koshu. Nama (unpasteurised) is sometimes described as 'raw', but that is not daiginjō.":"「純粹的米」是純米，「熟成的清酒」是古酒。生酒（未經加熱殺菌）有時被形容為「生的」，但那不是大吟釀。","Which sakes usually have cereal and lactic aromas with more acidity and umami?":"哪些清酒通常有穀物和乳酸香氣，酸度和鮮味也較多？","Junmai and honjōzō":"純米和本釀造","Ginjō and daiginjō":"吟釀和大吟釀","Junmai ginjō and junmai daiginjō":"純米吟釀和純米大吟釀","Daiginjō and junmai daiginjō":"大吟釀和純米大吟釀","Junmai and honjōzō make up the cereal, lactic family.":"純米和本釀造構成穀物、乳酸家族。","All the other options contain the word ginjō: they belong to the fruity, floral style with less acidity and umami.":"其他選項都含有「吟釀」這個詞：它們屬於果香、花香風格，酸度和鮮味較少。","What does junmai mean?":"「純米」是什麼意思？","Pure rice: no distilled alcohol added":"純粹的米：沒有添加蒸餾酒精","Rice polished to 50%":"精米到 50% 的米","Unfiltered sake":"未過濾的清酒","Unpasteurised sake":"未經加熱殺菌的清酒","Junmai means 'pure rice': no distilled alcohol was added before filtration.":"純米的意思是「純粹的米」：過濾前沒有添加蒸餾酒精。","50% is the daiginjō threshold. All sake is filtered by law; unpasteurised sake is called nama.":"50% 是大吟釀的門檻。依法所有清酒都要過濾；未經加熱殺菌的清酒稱為生酒。","What is the ONLY difference between a ginjō and a junmai ginjō?":"吟釀和純米吟釀之間唯一的差別是什麼？","The junmai ginjō has no added distilled alcohol":"純米吟釀沒有添加蒸餾酒精","The junmai ginjō is polished to 70%":"純米吟釀精米到 70%","The junmai ginjō is cloudy":"純米吟釀是混濁的","The junmai ginjō is aged":"純米吟釀經過熟成","The style is similar; only the added alcohol differs, absent in junmai ginjō.":"風格相似；唯一不同的是添加的酒精，純米吟釀沒有。","Both require rice polished to 60% or less. Cloudiness (nigori) and ageing (koshu) are speciality styles, not the difference between these grades.":"兩者都要求精米到 60% 以下。混濁（濁酒）和熟成（古酒）是特殊風格，不是這兩個等級的差別。","A sake is polished to 65% and had distilled alcohol added before filtration. Which grade can it carry?":"一款清酒精米到 65%，過濾前添加了蒸餾酒精。它可以標示哪個等級？","With added alcohol and polishing of 70% or less (but above 60%), the grade is honjōzō.":"添加了酒精、精米 70% 以下（但高於 60%），等級就是本釀造。","Ginjō would need 60% or less. Junmai and junmai ginjō rule out added alcohol.":"吟釀需要 60% 以下。純米和純米吟釀不能添加酒精。","A sake is polished to 45% with no added alcohol. What is the highest grade it can carry?":"一款清酒精米到 45%，沒有添加酒精。它最高可以標示哪個等級？","Rice at 50% or less and no added alcohol: junmai daiginjō.":"米在 50% 以下且沒有添加酒精：純米大吟釀。","Daiginjō and honjōzō mean added alcohol. Junmai ginjō would be possible, but it is not the highest grade for rice at 45%.":"大吟釀和本釀造代表有添加酒精。純米吟釀也可以，但對於 45% 的米來說不是最高等級。","How many of the six main premium grades contain the word junmai?":"六個主要高級等級中，有幾個含有「純米」這個詞？","Six":"六個","Junmai, junmai ginjō and junmai daiginjō: three grades with no added alcohol.":"純米、純米吟釀和純米大吟釀：三個沒有添加酒精的等級。","The other three (honjōzō, ginjō, daiginjō) have distilled alcohol added before filtration.":"另外三個（本釀造、吟釀、大吟釀）在過濾前添加了蒸餾酒精。","A customer wants a premium sake with pure fruity, floral aromas, made from rice only with no added alcohol. What do you suggest?":"一位客人想要一款帶有純淨果香、花香的高級清酒，只用米釀造、沒有添加酒精。你會推薦什麼？","Junmai ginjō or junmai daiginjō":"純米吟釀或純米大吟釀","Ginjō or daiginjō":"吟釀或大吟釀","Ginjō = fruit and flowers; junmai = no added alcohol: both words are needed.":"吟釀 = 果香和花香；純米 = 沒有添加酒精：兩個詞都需要。","Ginjō and daiginjō without junmai have added alcohol. Honjōzō has added alcohol and a cereal style; futsū-shu is not premium.":"沒有「純米」的吟釀和大吟釀有添加酒精。本釀造有添加酒精，而且是穀物風格；普通酒不是高級清酒。","Which combination of factors gives the junmai and honjōzō style?":"哪種因素組合會帶來純米和本釀造的風格？","Lightly polished rice and warmer fermentation":"輕度精米和較溫暖的發酵","Highly polished rice and cool fermentation":"高度精米和低溫發酵","Long ageing and rough filtration":"長時間熟成和粗過濾","No pasteurisation and carbon dioxide":"不加熱殺菌和二氧化碳","Light polishing and warmer temperature give cereal, lactic notes, acidity and umami.":"輕度精米和較高的溫度帶來穀物、乳酸調性、酸度和鮮味。","Highly polished rice and cool fermentation are the ginjō recipe. The other options describe speciality styles (koshu, nigori, nama, sparkling).":"高度精米和低溫發酵是吟釀的配方。其他選項描述的是特殊風格（古酒、濁酒、生酒、氣泡清酒）。","Which production step is legally controlled for junmai?":"哪個生產步驟對純米有法律規範？","No addition of distilled alcohol":"不添加蒸餾酒精","No pasteurisation":"不加熱殺菌","Compulsory ageing":"強制熟成","Rough filtration":"粗過濾","By law, junmai may not have high-strength distilled alcohol added.":"依法，純米不得添加高濃度蒸餾酒精。","No pasteurisation defines nama, ageing defines koshu, and rough filtration defines nigori.":"不加熱殺菌定義生酒，熟成定義古酒，粗過濾定義濁酒。","Which production step defines koshu?":"哪個生產步驟定義了古酒？","Maturation (ageing)":"熟成（陳放）","Adding carbon dioxide":"添加二氧化碳","Polishing to 50%":"精米到 50%","Koshu is aged sake: maturation is the step that defines it.":"古酒是熟成的清酒：熟成就是定義它的步驟。","No pasteurisation = nama; carbon dioxide = sparkling; 50% polishing = daiginjō.":"不加熱殺菌 = 生酒；二氧化碳 = 氣泡清酒；精米 50% = 大吟釀。","What is the maximum polishing ratio for honjōzō?":"本釀造的最高精米步合是多少？","For honjōzō, 70% or less of the grain must remain.":"本釀造必須保留 70% 以下的米粒。","50% and 60% are the daiginjō and ginjō limits. 100% is allowed only for junmai, and even there it is rare.":"50% 和 60% 是大吟釀和吟釀的上限。100% 只有純米允許，而且即使是純米也很少見。","Which of these sakes has no added distilled alcohol at all?":"下列哪一款清酒完全沒有添加蒸餾酒精？","The word junmai guarantees no added distilled alcohol.":"「純米」這個詞保證沒有添加蒸餾酒精。","Daiginjō, ginjō and honjōzō without the word junmai are sakes with alcohol added before filtration.":"沒有「純米」字樣的大吟釀、吟釀和本釀造，都是在過濾前添加了酒精的清酒。","Which pair of grades shares the same minimum polishing and differs only in added alcohol?":"哪一對等級的最低精米程度相同，只差在有沒有添加酒精？","Honjōzō and ginjō":"本釀造和吟釀","Junmai ginjō and daiginjō":"純米吟釀和大吟釀","Futsū-shu and junmai daiginjō":"普通酒和純米大吟釀","Both need rice at 50% or less; only the junmai version has no added alcohol.":"兩者都需要 50% 以下的米；只有純米版本沒有添加酒精。","Honjōzō (70%) and ginjō (60%) have different limits, as do junmai ginjō (60%) and daiginjō (50%). Futsū-shu is not a premium grade.":"本釀造（70%）和吟釀（60%）的上限不同，純米吟釀（60%）和大吟釀（50%）也是。普通酒不是高級等級。","A premium sake smells of cereal and yoghurt, with lively acidity and plenty of umami. Which family does it most likely belong to?":"一款高級清酒聞起來有穀物和優格的香氣，酸度活潑、鮮味豐富。它最可能屬於哪個家族？","Junmai or honjōzō":"純米或本釀造","Cereal, lactic notes, more acidity and umami: that is the junmai and honjōzō style.":"穀物、乳酸調性，酸度和鮮味較多：這就是純米和本釀造的風格。","Every other option contains the word ginjō: those sakes are fruity and floral, with less acidity and umami.":"其他選項都含有「吟釀」這個詞：那些清酒果香、花香明顯，酸度和鮮味較少。","Which statement about daiginjō is true?":"關於大吟釀，哪一項敘述正確？","Rice at 50% or less and the purest fruity, floral aromas":"米在 50% 以下，帶有最純淨的果香和花香","It never contains added distilled alcohol":"它從不添加蒸餾酒精","It is a basic, non-premium sake":"它是基本的非高級清酒","It is always cloudy and sparkling":"它一定是混濁且有氣泡的","Daiginjō, the 'big ginjō': highly polished rice and very pure aromas.":"大吟釀，「大的吟釀」：高度精米，香氣非常純淨。","Without the word junmai, a daiginjō has had distilled alcohol added. It is a premium grade; cloudy and sparkling describe speciality styles, not this grade.":"沒有「純米」字樣的大吟釀添加了蒸餾酒精。它是高級等級；混濁和氣泡描述的是特殊風格，不是這個等級。","Which of these sakes is NOT a premium grade?":"下列哪一款清酒不是高級等級？","Futsū-shu is basic sake; the other three are premium grades.":"普通酒是基本清酒；其他三個是高級等級。","Honjōzō, junmai and ginjō are among the six main premium grades, each with its own production rules.":"本釀造、純米和吟釀都屬於六個主要高級等級，各有自己的生產規定。","Rice polished to 55% and distilled alcohol added before filtration: what is the highest grade possible?":"米精米到 55%，過濾前添加了蒸餾酒精：最高可能是哪個等級？","55% meets the ginjō limit (60% or less) but not the daiginjō limit (50%).":"55% 符合吟釀的上限（60% 以下），但不符合大吟釀的上限（50%）。","The two junmai grades rule out added alcohol. Daiginjō would need rice at 50% or less.":"兩個純米等級不能添加酒精。大吟釀則需要 50% 以下的米。","Compared with ginjō styles, junmai and honjōzō usually have…":"與吟釀風格相比，純米和本釀造通常…","more acidity and umami":"酸度和鮮味較多","less acidity and umami":"酸度和鮮味較少","much more alcohol":"酒精多很多","an amber colour":"呈琥珀色","The cereal, lactic style comes with more acidity and umami.":"穀物、乳酸風格伴隨較多的酸度和鮮味。","Less acidity and umami is the ginjō profile. Strength and colour are similar across nearly all premium sakes.":"酸度和鮮味較少是吟釀的特徵。幾乎所有高級清酒的酒精濃度和顏色都相近。","How many kanji must you learn to recognise the four key label terms?":"要認得四個關鍵酒標用語，必須學會幾個漢字？","Ten":"十個","There are seven, because gin 吟 and jō 醸 appear in more than one term.":"七個，因為吟（gin）和醸（jō）出現在不只一個用語中。","Writing out the four terms takes ten symbols in all; but jō appears three times and gin twice, so there are only seven different symbols.":"四個用語全部寫出來共有十個字；但醸出現三次、吟出現兩次，所以不同的字只有七個。","Which term is written 純米?":"哪個用語寫作「純米」？","純 (jun) + 米 (mai) = junmai, 'pure rice'.":"純（jun）+ 米（mai）= junmai，「純粹的米」。","Ginjō is 吟醸, honjōzō is 本醸造, daiginjō is 大吟醸.":"ginjō 是吟醸，honjōzō 是本醸造，daiginjō 是大吟醸。","A label shows 大吟醸. Which grade is it?":"酒標上寫著「大吟醸」。這是哪個等級？","Ginjō would be just 吟醸, without 大. Junmai is 純米 and honjōzō 本醸造.":"吟釀只寫「吟醸」，沒有「大」。純米寫作「純米」，本釀造寫作「本醸造」。","Which kanji appears in both ginjō and honjōzō?":"哪個漢字同時出現在 ginjō 和 honjōzō 中？","The sound jō, 醸, is in ginjō (吟醸), honjōzō (本醸造) and daiginjō (大吟醸).":"讀作 jō 的「醸」出現在 ginjō（吟醸）、honjōzō（本醸造）和 daiginjō（大吟醸）中。","吟 (gin) is in ginjō and daiginjō but not honjōzō. 米 is only in junmai and 大 only in daiginjō.":"吟（gin）出現在 ginjō 和 daiginjō，但不在 honjōzō。米只在 junmai，大只在 daiginjō。","A label shows 純米吟醸. What does it indicate?":"酒標上寫著「純米吟醸」。這代表什麼？","Junmai ginjō: fruity style, no added alcohol":"純米吟釀：果香風格，沒有添加酒精","Honjōzō: cereal style, with added alcohol":"本釀造：穀物風格，有添加酒精","Daiginjō: with added alcohol, rice at 50%":"大吟釀：有添加酒精，米為 50%","Futsū-shu: basic sake":"普通酒：基本清酒","純米 (junmai) + 吟醸 (ginjō): fruity and floral, with no added distilled alcohol.":"純米（junmai）+ 吟醸（ginjō）：果香、花香，沒有添加蒸餾酒精。","There is no 本醸造 (honjōzō) and no 大 (dai). Futsū-shu does not carry these premium terms.":"沒有本醸造（honjōzō），也沒有大（dai）。普通酒不會標示這些高級用語。","What does 純米 mean?":"「純米」是什麼意思？","Junmai (pure rice)":"純米（純粹的米）","純米 reads junmai and means 'pure rice'.":"純米讀作 junmai，意思是「純粹的米」。","Ginjō is written 吟醸. Nama and koshu are not among the four kanji terms at this level.":"吟釀寫作「吟醸」。生酒和古酒不在這個程度要學的四個漢字用語之中。","A label shows 本醸造. What do you know about this sake?":"酒標上寫著「本醸造」。關於這款清酒你知道什麼？","It is honjōzō: added alcohol, rice ≤70%, cereal style":"它是本釀造：有添加酒精，米 ≤70%，穀物風格","It is junmai: no added alcohol":"它是純米：沒有添加酒精","It is daiginjō: rice ≤50%":"它是大吟釀：米 ≤50%","It is an unpasteurised sake":"它是未經加熱殺菌的清酒","本醸造 = honjōzō: added alcohol, polished to 70% or less, cereal and lactic notes.":"本醸造 = honjōzō：有添加酒精，精米到 70% 以下，穀物和乳酸調性。","Junmai is 純米 and daiginjō 大吟醸. Unpasteurised sake is called nama.":"純米寫作「純米」，大吟釀寫作「大吟醸」。未經加熱殺菌的清酒稱為生酒。","In which term does the kanji 米 (mai) appear?":"漢字「米」（mai）出現在哪個用語中？","米 is the 'mai' in junmai (純米).":"米就是 junmai（純米）中的「mai」。","Ginjō (吟醸), honjōzō (本醸造) and daiginjō (大吟醸) do not contain 米.":"ginjō（吟醸）、honjōzō（本醸造）和 daiginjō（大吟醸）都不含「米」。","Why is it useful to recognise the kanji of the four key terms?":"為什麼認得四個關鍵用語的漢字很有用？","Labels are mostly in Japanese, with few words in the Latin alphabet":"酒標大多是日文，很少有拉丁字母","The kanji show the sake's price":"漢字會標示清酒的價格","The kanji show the production year":"漢字會標示生產年份","The law bans the Latin alphabet":"法律禁止使用拉丁字母","Recognising a few kanji lets you read the grade even on an all-Japanese label.":"認得幾個漢字，就算酒標全是日文也能看出等級。","These kanji show the grade, not price or year; the Latin alphabet is not banned, just little used.":"這些漢字標示的是等級，不是價格或年份；拉丁字母並沒有被禁止，只是很少使用。","How is ginjō written?":"ginjō 怎麼寫？","純米 is junmai and 本醸造 honjōzō. 大吟醸 is daiginjō: the same two kanji with 大 (dai) in front.":"純米是 junmai，本醸造是 honjōzō。大吟醸是 daiginjō：同樣兩個漢字，前面加上大（dai）。","A label reads 純米大吟醸. What is in the bottle?":"酒標上寫著「純米大吟醸」。瓶子裡是什麼？","Junmai daiginjō: rice at 50% or less, no added alcohol":"純米大吟釀：米在 50% 以下，沒有添加酒精","Daiginjō: rice at 50% or less, with added alcohol":"大吟釀：米在 50% 以下，有添加酒精","Junmai ginjō: rice at 60% or less, no added alcohol":"純米吟釀：米在 60% 以下，沒有添加酒精","Honjōzō: rice at 70% or less, with added alcohol":"本釀造：米在 70% 以下，有添加酒精","純米 (junmai) + 大吟醸 (daiginjō): the two terms add up.":"純米（junmai）+ 大吟醸（daiginjō）：兩個用語疊加。","Without 純米 it would be a daiginjō with added alcohol; without 大, a junmai ginjō. Honjōzō is written 本醸造.":"沒有「純米」就是添加了酒精的大吟釀；沒有「大」就是純米吟釀。本釀造寫作「本醸造」。","Which kanji means 'big' and tells a daiginjō from a ginjō on a label?":"哪個漢字的意思是「大」，能在酒標上區分大吟釀和吟釀？","大 reads dai: 大吟醸 is the 'big ginjō'.":"「大」讀作 dai：大吟醸就是「大的吟釀」。","純 is the jun of junmai; 本 and 造 are the first and last kanji of honjōzō.":"純是 junmai 的 jun；本和造是 honjōzō 的第一個和最後一個漢字。","What does the term nama indicate?":"「生」（nama）這個用語代表什麼？","An unpasteurised sake":"未經加熱殺菌的清酒","A cloudy sake":"混濁的清酒","An aged sake":"熟成的清酒","A sake with no added alcohol":"沒有添加酒精的清酒","Nama means the sake has skipped pasteurisation.":"「生」表示這款清酒省略了加熱殺菌。","Cloudy is nigori, aged is koshu, no added alcohol is junmai.":"混濁是濁酒，熟成是古酒，沒有添加酒精是純米。","How should a nama-zake be stored?":"生酒（nama-zake）應該怎麼保存？","Always refrigerated, drunk soon after release":"一直冷藏，上市後盡快飲用","At room temperature for a few years":"常溫存放幾年","Lying down, in sunlight":"平放，放在陽光下","In an oak cask":"放在橡木桶中","Without pasteurisation nama is less stable: constant cold and quick drinking.":"沒有加熱殺菌，生酒比較不穩定：要一直冷藏並盡快喝掉。","Heat and time spoil it, and strong light harms any sake. Sake bottles are stored upright, and oak has nothing to do with it.":"熱和時間會讓它變質，強光對任何清酒都有害。清酒瓶要直立存放，與橡木無關。","What aromas can a nama quickly develop?":"生酒很快會出現什麼香氣？","Spicy and malty":"辛香和麥芽味","White flowers and pear":"白花和西洋梨","Citrus and mint":"柑橘和薄荷","On release it is fresh and lively, but it can soon turn spicy and malty.":"剛上市時新鮮活潑，但很快就可能變得帶有辛香和麥芽味。","Flowers and pear are ginjō notes, not linked to nama. Vanilla and coconut come from oak, which is hardly used for sake.":"花香和西洋梨是吟釀的調性，與生酒無關。香草和椰子來自橡木，而清酒幾乎不用橡木。","Why is a nigori cloudy?":"濁酒為什麼是混濁的？","It is roughly filtered and contains rice particles":"它經過粗過濾，含有米粒","It has never been filtered":"它從未過濾","Milk is added to it":"裡面加了牛奶","It is aged in terracotta":"它在陶甕中熟成","Rough filtration leaves suspended rice particles in the sake.":"粗過濾讓米粒懸浮在清酒中。","Filtration is legally required: nigori is filtered, just lightly. No milk is used, and terracotta relates to some koshu.":"過濾是法律規定的：濁酒有過濾，只是過濾得比較輕。沒有用牛奶，陶甕則與部分古酒有關。","Which statement about nigori is correct?":"關於濁酒，哪一項敘述正確？","It can be lightly cloudy or thick with rice fragments":"可以只是微濁，也可以濃稠並帶有米粒碎片","It always turns clear after a few days":"幾天後一定會變清澈","It is always sparkling":"一定有氣泡","It is always a futsū-shu":"一定是普通酒","The style varies with how much solid matter is kept: from slightly cloudy to very thick.":"風格隨保留的固形物多寡而不同：從微濁到非常濃稠。","A nigori can be sparkling, but need not be. Speciality styles combine with any grade, not just futsū-shu.":"濁酒可以有氣泡，但不一定。特殊風格可以和任何等級結合，不只是普通酒。","Where do the bubbles in sparkling sake come from?":"氣泡清酒的氣泡從哪裡來？","Carbon dioxide dissolved in the liquid":"溶在液體中的二氧化碳","Oxygen from the air":"空氣中的氧氣","The added distilled alcohol":"添加的蒸餾酒精","The rice particles":"米粒","As in any sparkling drink, they are dissolved carbon dioxide.":"和任何氣泡飲料一樣，氣泡是溶解的二氧化碳。","Oxygen spoils sake rather than making it fizzy. Rice particles make it cloudy (nigori), not sparkling.":"氧氣會讓清酒變質，而不是讓它起泡。米粒讓清酒混濁（濁酒），而不是起泡。","Which description of sparkling sake is correct?":"關於氣泡清酒，哪一項描述正確？","It ranges from light and low-alcohol to rich and complex, and can be nigori":"從清淡低酒精到濃郁複雜都有，也可以是濁酒","It is always 20% abv":"一定是 20% abv","It is made with a single technique":"只用單一種技術製作","It can never be cloudy":"絕不會混濁","Styles vary: some light and low in alcohol, others richer, even cloudy.":"風格多樣：有些清淡、酒精低，有些較濃郁，甚至是混濁的。","Several techniques exist for dissolving carbon dioxide. Many sparkling sakes are lighter, not 20%, and some are also nigori.":"讓二氧化碳溶入的技術有好幾種。許多氣泡清酒比較清淡，不是 20%，有些也是濁酒。","What is a koshu?":"古酒是什麼？","A sparkling sake":"氣泡清酒","A basic sake":"基本清酒","Koshu is the name for aged sakes.":"古酒是熟成清酒的名稱。","Sparkling, unpasteurised (nama) and basic (futsū-shu) are other categories.":"氣泡、未經加熱殺菌（生酒）和基本（普通酒）是其他類別。","Which vessel is VERY RARELY used to age sake?":"哪種容器「非常少」用來熟成清酒？","The oak cask":"橡木桶","The storage tank":"儲存槽","The glass bottle":"玻璃瓶","Terracotta":"陶甕","Wood can be used, but oak is very rare for sake.":"木材可以使用，但清酒很少用橡木。","Tanks, glass and terracotta are all used for ageing koshu.":"槽、玻璃和陶甕都用來熟成古酒。","What is a typical koshu like?":"典型的古酒是什麼樣子？","Amber or brown, rich, sweet, with dried fruit and nut notes":"琥珀色或褐色、濃郁、甜，帶有果乾和堅果調性","Colourless, very light, with green apple notes":"無色、非常清淡，帶有青蘋果調性","Cloudy, with rice fragments":"混濁，帶有米粒碎片","Sparkling and low in alcohol":"有氣泡、酒精低","Most koshu is amber or brown, rich, sweet and highly aromatic.":"大多數古酒呈琥珀色或褐色，濃郁、甜，香氣非常豐富。","Paler, more delicate koshu exist, but they are not the norm. Cloudy suggests nigori and fizzy suggests sparkling sake.":"也有顏色較淡、較細緻的古酒，但不是常態。混濁讓人想到濁酒，有氣泡則是氣泡清酒。","Why do koshu vary so much?":"為什麼古酒的差異這麼大？","They are aged in different vessels and at different temperatures, even below freezing":"它們在不同容器、不同溫度下熟成，甚至在冰點以下","They are made from different grape varieties":"它們用不同的葡萄品種釀造","They are distilled several times":"它們經過多次蒸餾","They contain different amounts of hops":"它們含有不同分量的啤酒花","Vessels (tanks, terracotta, glass, wood) and temperatures (room or low) create many styles.":"容器（槽、陶甕、玻璃、木材）和溫度（常溫或低溫）創造出許多風格。","Sake is made from rice, not grapes or hops, and is not distilled.":"清酒是用米釀造的，不是葡萄或啤酒花，也不經蒸餾。","Can a junmai ginjō also be nama and nigori?":"純米吟釀也可以同時是生酒和濁酒嗎？","Yes: speciality styles combine with any grade":"可以：特殊風格能和任何等級結合","No: speciality styles apply only to futsū-shu":"不行：特殊風格只適用於普通酒","No: a sake can have only one speciality style":"不行：一款清酒只能有一種特殊風格","Only if it is also aged":"只有在它也熟成的情況下才可以","A premium sake can be nama, nigori, sparkling or koshu, even combining several.":"高級清酒可以是生酒、濁酒、氣泡清酒或古酒，甚至結合好幾種。","Speciality styles are not reserved for futsū-shu and do not exclude each other; ageing is not a condition.":"特殊風格不是普通酒專屬，彼此也不互斥；熟成不是條件。","Which four speciality styles should you know at this level?":"在這個程度你應該認識哪四種特殊風格？","Nama, nigori, sparkling sake, koshu":"生酒、濁酒、氣泡清酒、古酒","Unpasteurised, cloudy, bubbly, aged: nama, nigori, sparkling, koshu.":"未經加熱殺菌、混濁、有氣泡、熟成：生酒、濁酒、氣泡清酒、古酒。","Junmai, honjōzō, ginjō and daiginjō are premium grades; futsū-shu is basic sake. Tokkuri, masu and o-choko are serving vessels.":"純米、本釀造、吟釀和大吟釀是高級等級；普通酒是基本清酒。德利、木升和豬口是侍酒器具。","Which speciality style comes from SKIPPING a production step?":"哪種特殊風格來自「省略」某個生產步驟？","Sparkling sake":"氣泡清酒","Nama skips pasteurisation.":"生酒省略了加熱殺菌。","Koshu adds a step, ageing; sparkling sake has carbon dioxide dissolved in it. Daiginjō is a grade, not a speciality style.":"古酒增加了熟成這一步；氣泡清酒溶有二氧化碳。大吟釀是等級，不是特殊風格。","What is a nama usually like when it has just been released?":"剛上市的生酒通常是什麼樣子？","Particularly lively and fresh":"特別活潑、新鮮","Rich, sweet, with nutty notes":"濃郁、甜，帶有堅果調性","Amber and very thick":"琥珀色而且非常濃稠","Without any aroma":"完全沒有香氣","With no pasteurisation, nama is released tasting especially lively and fresh.":"沒有加熱殺菌，生酒上市時嘗起來特別活潑、新鮮。","Rich, sweet and amber sounds more like koshu. Nama's freshness does not last: hence the fridge and drinking it soon.":"濃郁、甜、琥珀色比較像古酒。生酒的新鮮感不會持久：所以要冷藏並盡快喝。","Why is it usually spelled nama-zake rather than nama-sake?":"為什麼通常拼作 nama-zake，而不是 nama-sake？","In Japanese the 's' softens to a 'z' in this word":"在日文中，這個詞裡的「s」會變成「z」","Zake is a different drink, made from barley":"zake 是另一種用大麥做的飲料","Zake means 'aged'":"zake 的意思是「熟成的」","It is the name of the region of origin":"那是產區的名稱","It is just pronunciation: nama-zake is unpasteurised sake.":"只是發音的關係：nama-zake 就是未經加熱殺菌的清酒（生酒）。","Zake is neither another drink nor a place, and aged sake is called koshu.":"zake 既不是另一種飲料也不是地名，而熟成的清酒稱為古酒。","A sake looks milky white with tiny fragments of rice. Which style is it?":"一款清酒呈乳白色，帶有細小的米粒碎片。這是哪種風格？","Cloudy from suspended rice particles: it is a nigori.":"懸浮的米粒造成混濁：這是濁酒。","Koshu is known by its amber or brown colour, nama by its lack of pasteurisation, which you cannot see. Honjōzō is a grade, normally clear.":"古酒以琥珀色或褐色辨認，生酒則是沒有加熱殺菌，這是看不出來的。本釀造是等級，通常是清澈的。","Which speciality style is typically amber or brown?":"哪種特殊風格通常呈琥珀色或褐色？","Ageing gives koshu its amber or brown colour.":"熟成讓古酒呈現琥珀色或褐色。","Nigori is cloudy and whitish; nama and sparkling are not defined by colour. In a sake that is not aged, a deeper colour points to a fault instead.":"濁酒是混濁偏白的；生酒和氣泡清酒不以顏色定義。在未熟成的清酒中，顏色變深反而代表缺陷。","Besides dried fruit and nuts, which aromas can a typical koshu show?":"除了果乾和堅果，典型的古酒還可能有哪些香氣？","Meat and pickled vegetables":"肉和醃菜","Banana and clove":"香蕉和丁香","Fresh grass and lime":"青草和萊姆","Koshu has pronounced aromas: dried fruit, nuts and even meat and pickles.":"古酒香氣明顯：果乾、堅果，甚至有肉和醃菜。","Vanilla and coconut come from oak, which is very rarely used for sake. The other notes do not describe an aged sake.":"香草和椰子來自橡木，而清酒很少用橡木。其他調性都不是熟成清酒的描述。","Which statement about koshu is true?":"關於古酒，哪一項敘述正確？","Most are amber and rich, but paler, more delicate ones exist":"大多數呈琥珀色、濃郁，但也有顏色較淡、較細緻的","They are all colourless":"全部都是無色的","They must be aged in oak casks":"必須在橡木桶中熟成","They must be drunk within a few months":"必須在幾個月內喝掉","Typical koshu is amber or brown, rich and sweet; some are paler and more delicate.":"典型的古酒呈琥珀色或褐色、濃郁而甜；有些顏色較淡、較細緻。","Oak is very rarely used: tanks, terracotta and glass bottles are. Drinking within a few months applies to nama, not to an aged sake.":"橡木很少使用：常用的是槽、陶甕和玻璃瓶。幾個月內喝掉適用於生酒，不是熟成清酒。","Which two speciality styles, together in one bottle, give a cloudy sake with bubbles?":"哪兩種特殊風格同時在一瓶中，會得到有氣泡的混濁清酒？","Nigori and sparkling":"濁酒和氣泡清酒","Nama and koshu":"生酒和古酒","Koshu and nigori":"古酒和濁酒","Nama and sparkling":"生酒和氣泡清酒","Nigori = cloudy; sparkling = bubbles. Speciality styles can be combined.":"濁酒 = 混濁；氣泡清酒 = 氣泡。特殊風格可以結合。","Nama only means unpasteurised and koshu aged: neither makes a sake cloudy or fizzy.":"生酒只表示未經加熱殺菌，古酒表示熟成：兩者都不會讓清酒混濁或起泡。","Which pairing of speciality style and production step is correct?":"哪一組特殊風格與生產步驟的配對正確？","Nama: no pasteurisation; koshu: ageing":"生酒：不加熱殺菌；古酒：熟成","Nama: ageing; koshu: no pasteurisation":"生酒：熟成；古酒：不加熱殺菌","Nama: rough filtration; koshu: bubbles":"生酒：粗過濾；古酒：氣泡","Nama: no added alcohol; koshu: polishing to 50%":"生酒：不添加酒精；古酒：精米到 50%","Nama = unpasteurised; koshu = aged.":"生酒 = 未經加熱殺菌；古酒 = 熟成。","Rough filtration defines nigori and bubbles sparkling sake. No added alcohol is junmai; 50% is the daiginjō limit.":"粗過濾定義濁酒，氣泡定義氣泡清酒。不添加酒精是純米；50% 是大吟釀的上限。","A bottle says 'futsū-shu nama'. What does that mean?":"酒瓶上寫著「普通酒 生」（futsū-shu nama）。這是什麼意思？","A basic sake that is unpasteurised":"未經加熱殺菌的基本清酒","A premium sake with no added alcohol":"沒有添加酒精的高級清酒","An aged sake polished to 50%":"精米到 50% 的熟成清酒","A cloudy, sparkling sake":"混濁的氣泡清酒","Futsū-shu = basic sake; nama = unpasteurised. Speciality styles apply to basic sake too.":"普通酒 = 基本清酒；生 = 未經加熱殺菌。特殊風格也適用於基本清酒。","No added alcohol would be junmai, aged would be koshu, cloudy would be nigori: none of those words is on the bottle.":"沒有添加酒精會是純米，熟成會是古酒，混濁會是濁酒：瓶上沒有這些字。","Which glass is best for tasting sake?":"哪種杯子最適合品飲清酒？","A wide bowl that narrows towards the top":"杯身寬、向杯口收窄的杯子","A straight, narrow flute":"直而窄的笛形杯","A wide, shallow, open coupe":"寬淺開口的碟形杯","A cedar wood box":"杉木盒","A shape that narrows at the top concentrates the aromas.":"杯口收窄的形狀能集中香氣。","An open coupe lets aromas escape. A cedar masu adds wood flavour and is unsuitable for tasting.":"開口的碟形杯會讓香氣散失。杉木製的木升會增加木頭味，不適合品飲。","Why is a white surface needed when tasting?":"品飲時為什麼需要白色的表面？","To assess the sake's appearance":"為了評估清酒的外觀","To cool the glass":"為了讓杯子降溫","To absorb smells":"為了吸收氣味","To warm the sake":"為了溫熱清酒","Clarity, colour and bubbles show up best against white.":"在白色背景下最能看清清澈度、顏色和氣泡。","The surface changes neither temperature nor smells: it is only for looking.":"表面既不改變溫度也不改變氣味：只是用來觀察。","What is assessed under 'appearance' when tasting?":"品飲時「外觀」要評估什麼？","Clarity (clear–cloudy), bubbles, colour":"清澈度（清澈–混濁）、氣泡、顏色","Sweetness and acidity":"甜度和酸度","Umami and finish":"鮮味和餘韻","Aroma families":"香氣類別","Appearance covers what you see: clarity, bubbles and colour.":"外觀包含你看到的：清澈度、氣泡和顏色。","Sweetness, acidity, umami and finish are judged on the palate; aroma families on the nose (and palate).":"甜度、酸度、鮮味和餘韻是在口中判斷；香氣類別在鼻聞（和口中）判斷。","Which of these aroma families is NOT part of the sake tasting grid?":"下列哪個香氣類別不屬於清酒品飲表？","Oak and vanilla":"橡木和香草","Fruity/floral":"果香／花香","Cereal/grain/nut":"穀物／穀粒／堅果","Lactic/dairy":"乳酸／乳製品","The grid uses fruity/floral, cereal/grain/nut, lactic/dairy and 'other'; oak is not one of the families.":"品飲表使用果香／花香、穀物／穀粒／堅果、乳酸／乳製品和「其他」；橡木不是其中的類別。","Oak is hardly used for sake, so it has no family of its own; the other three options are grid families.":"清酒幾乎不用橡木，所以沒有自己的類別；其他三個選項都是品飲表的類別。","Before a tasting, what should you avoid just beforehand?":"品飲前，應該避免在品飲前剛做什麼？","Brushing your teeth or drinking coffee":"刷牙或喝咖啡","Drinking a glass of water":"喝一杯水","Switching on a light":"開燈","Sitting at a white table":"坐在白色桌子前","Toothpaste and coffee leave lingering flavours that distort tasting.":"牙膏和咖啡會留下殘留的味道，干擾品飲。","Water leaves no taste, light helps you see, and a white table is actually recommended.":"水不會留下味道，光線幫助觀察，而白色桌子其實是建議的。","What are the four rules for storing sake?":"保存清酒的四個原則是什麼？","Keep cool, drink young, store upright, avoid bright light":"保持涼爽、趁年輕喝、直立存放、避開強光","Keep warm, age it, lie it down, keep in light":"保持溫暖、讓它熟成、平放、放在光線下","Keep cool, age it, lie it down, keep dark":"保持涼爽、讓它熟成、平放、放在暗處","Room temperature, drink young, lie it down, keep in light":"常溫、趁年輕喝、平放、放在光線下","Cool, young, upright, away from bright light.":"涼爽、年輕、直立、避開強光。","Sake is not stored lying down and, apart from koshu, is not made for ageing; heat and bright light spoil it.":"清酒不平放，而且除了古酒以外都不是為熟成而釀的；熱和強光會讓它變質。","Within what time should most sake be drunk after going on sale?":"大多數清酒上市後應該在多久內喝完？","About a year":"約一年","There is no limit":"沒有期限","Most sake should be drunk within about a year, after which it loses freshness.":"大多數清酒應在約一年內喝完，之後會失去新鮮感。","A week is how long an opened ginjō lasts. Nama lasts only a few months; only koshu is meant for ageing.":"一週是開瓶吟釀能保存的時間。生酒只能保存幾個月；只有古酒是為熟成而釀的。","How long does a ginjō usually stay fresh once opened, in the fridge?":"吟釀開瓶後放在冰箱，通常能保持新鮮多久？","About one week":"約一週","About two months":"約兩個月","About an hour":"約一小時","Delicate ginjō stays fresh for about a week; other sakes about two.":"細緻的吟釀約能保持新鮮一週；其他清酒約兩週。","Two months or a year is far too long for an opened bottle. An hour is too short: resealed in the fridge, sake keeps for days.":"兩個月或一年對開過的酒瓶來說太長了。一小時太短：重新封好放冰箱，清酒能保存好幾天。","How should sake bottles be stored?":"清酒瓶應該怎麼存放？","Upright":"直立","Lying down":"平放","Upside down":"倒放","Tilted at 45°":"傾斜 45°","Sake bottles are best stored upright.":"清酒瓶最好直立存放。","Lying bottles down is a habit for cork-sealed wine, not sake.":"平放酒瓶是軟木塞封瓶葡萄酒的習慣，不適用於清酒。","A sake bottle has been open too long: it is darker and smells of caramel and toffee. Which fault is this?":"一瓶清酒開瓶太久：顏色變深，聞起來有焦糖和太妃糖味。這是哪種缺陷？","Light strike":"光害","Bacterial infection":"細菌感染","Open too long + caramel, toffee and deeper colour = oxidation.":"開瓶太久 + 焦糖、太妃糖、顏色變深 = 氧化。","Nama-hine affects nama and smells of malt, meat and rotting vegetables. The other two are not the sake faults covered at this level.":"生老香（nama-hine）發生在生酒上，聞起來有麥芽、肉和腐爛蔬菜味。另外兩個不是這個程度所教的清酒缺陷。","Which aromas show a sake is 'out of condition', meaning too old?":"哪些香氣代表清酒「狀態不佳」，也就是太老了？","Caramel, toffee, pickled vegetables":"焦糖、太妃糖、醃菜","Green apple and white flowers":"青蘋果和白花","Steamed rice and yoghurt":"蒸米和優格","Fresh citrus":"新鮮柑橘","A bottle that is too old smells of caramel, toffee and pickles, and darkens.":"太老的酒瓶聞起來有焦糖、太妃糖和醃菜味，顏色也會變深。","Apple, flowers, rice, yoghurt and citrus are normal, welcome aromas in fresh sake.":"蘋果、花、米、優格和柑橘都是新鮮清酒中正常、討喜的香氣。","What is nama-hine?":"什麼是生老香（nama-hine）？","A fault in nama: malt, meat, rotting-vegetable aromas":"生酒的缺陷：麥芽、肉、腐爛蔬菜的氣味","A type of sparkling sake":"一種氣泡清酒","The traditional cup":"傳統的酒杯","A stage of kōji making":"製麴的一個階段","Nama-hine is the typical fault of unpasteurised sake.":"生老香是未經加熱殺菌清酒的典型缺陷。","The cup is the o-choko. Nama-hine is neither a style nor a production stage.":"酒杯是豬口。生老香既不是風格，也不是生產階段。","Why do oxidation aromas resemble those of koshu?":"為什麼氧化的香氣和古酒的香氣相似？","The same processes create them, but in koshu they are wanted":"它們由相同的過程產生，但在古酒中是刻意追求的","Koshu is always a faulty sake":"古酒一定是有缺陷的清酒","Koshu is made from oxidised rice":"古酒是用氧化的米做的","It is an unexplained coincidence":"這是無法解釋的巧合","Same processes, different intention: in koshu those aromas are part of the style.":"過程相同，意圖不同：在古酒中，這些香氣是風格的一部分。","Koshu is a deliberate style, not a fault, made from the same rice as other sakes: ageing creates those aromas.":"古酒是刻意的風格，不是缺陷，使用的米和其他清酒相同：是熟成創造了這些香氣。","Which sake most needs refrigeration?":"哪一款清酒最需要冷藏？","Nama-zake":"生酒","Koshu":"古酒","Pasteurised futsū-shu":"加熱殺菌過的普通酒","All sakes equally":"所有清酒都一樣","Nama is unpasteurised and less stable: it must be kept cold at all times.":"生酒未經加熱殺菌、比較不穩定：必須一直保持低溫。","Every sake prefers cool storage, but for nama the cold is essential; ginjō also benefits to protect its aromas.":"所有清酒都適合涼爽保存，但對生酒來說低溫是必要的；吟釀冷藏也有助於保護香氣。","What is the recommended serving temperature for ginjō styles?":"吟釀風格建議的侍酒溫度是多少？","Chilled (6–13 °C)":"冰涼（6–13 °C）","Warm (40–50 °C)":"溫熱（40–50 °C）","Very hot, above 60 °C":"非常燙，60 °C 以上","Below freezing":"冰點以下","Ginjō loses its aromas when heated: serve it chilled.":"吟釀加熱會失去香氣：要冰涼飲用。","Heat would flatten the fruity, floral scents. Below freezing the sake would be too cold to smell.":"加熱會讓果香、花香變得平淡。在冰點以下，清酒太冷而聞不到香氣。","Which temperature range is warm/hot sake?":"溫熱／熱的清酒是哪個溫度範圍？","Warm/hot sake is served at 40–50 °C.":"溫熱／熱的清酒在 40–50 °C 侍酒。","15–18 °C is room temperature and 6–13 °C chilled. About 80 °C is the water-bath water, not the sake.":"15–18 °C 是常溫，6–13 °C 是冰涼。約 80 °C 是隔水加熱的水溫，不是清酒的溫度。","What effect does warming have on a sake?":"加溫對清酒有什麼影響？","It seems more intense, richer and more acidic":"喝起來更強烈、更濃郁、酸度更明顯","It becomes lighter and less acidic":"變得更清淡、酸度更低","It raises its alcohol level":"提高酒精濃度","It boosts ginjō aromas":"增強吟釀香氣","Heat increases perceived intensity, texture and acidity, which can help with food.":"加熱會提高感受到的強度、口感和酸度，有助於搭配食物。","Strength does not change, and ginjō loses aromas with heat. The effect is the opposite of 'lighter'.":"酒精濃度不會改變，吟釀加熱會失去香氣。效果與「更清淡」相反。","Which sakes can be served chilled, at room temperature AND warm?":"哪些清酒可以冰涼、常溫「而且」溫熱飲用？","Futsū-shu, junmai and honjōzō":"普通酒、純米和本釀造","Sparkling sake only":"只有氣泡清酒","Futsū-shu, junmai and honjōzō suit all three temperatures.":"普通酒、純米和本釀造適合三種溫度。","No ginjō style (including junmai ginjō and junmai daiginjō) should be warmed. Sparkling is served chilled only.":"任何吟釀風格（包括純米吟釀和純米大吟釀）都不應加溫。氣泡清酒只能冰涼飲用。","How is sparkling sake served?":"氣泡清酒怎麼侍酒？","Chilled only":"只能冰涼","Warm, to feel the bubbles better":"溫熱，更能感受氣泡","At room temperature or warm":"常溫或溫熱","At any temperature":"任何溫度都可以","Sparkling sake is served chilled only.":"氣泡清酒只能冰涼飲用。","Neither room temperature nor warm suits sparkling sake, which would lose freshness and bubbles.":"常溫和溫熱都不適合氣泡清酒，會失去新鮮感和氣泡。","How are koshu and nigori generally served?":"古酒和濁酒一般怎麼侍酒？","Generally chilled, though some can be served warm":"一般冰涼，但有些可以溫熱飲用","Always warm":"一定溫熱","Never chilled":"絕不冰涼","Only at room temperature":"只能常溫","Koshu and nigori are generally best chilled, but some can be served warm.":"古酒和濁酒一般最適合冰涼，但有些可以溫熱飲用。","Options with 'always', 'never' or 'only' are too rigid for such varied styles.":"帶有「一定」、「絕不」或「只能」的選項，對這麼多樣的風格來說太死板。","To warm sake in a water bath, what temperature should the water be?":"用隔水加熱的方式溫清酒，水溫應該是多少？","About 80 °C":"約 80 °C","About 40 °C":"約 40 °C","Boiling, 100 °C, for 10 minutes":"沸騰，100 °C，加熱 10 分鐘","About 15 °C":"約 15 °C","Water at about 80 °C: a small tokkuri warms in 2–4 minutes.":"約 80 °C 的水：小德利 2–4 分鐘就能溫熱。","40 °C is the temperature of warm sake, not the water. Boiling water for 10 minutes would overheat it; 15 °C would not warm it at all.":"40 °C 是溫熱清酒的溫度，不是水溫。沸水加熱 10 分鐘會過熱；15 °C 則完全無法加溫。","What should always be used when warming sake in a water bath?":"隔水加熱清酒時一定要使用什麼？","A thermometer":"溫度計","A cedar masu":"杉木升","Ice":"冰塊","A wine glass":"葡萄酒杯","A thermometer keeps you in the right 40–50 °C range.":"溫度計能讓你保持在 40–50 °C 的正確範圍。","Sake is warmed in the tokkuri, not a masu or glass; ice is for cooling.":"清酒是在德利裡加溫，不是在木升或杯子裡；冰塊是用來降溫的。","About how long does it take to warm 180 mL of sake in a microwave?":"用微波爐加熱 180 mL 清酒大約需要多久？","20–40 seconds":"20–40 秒","2–4 minutes":"2–4 分鐘","5 seconds":"5 秒","10 minutes":"10 分鐘","About 20–40 seconds, depending on the microwave and target temperature.":"約 20–40 秒，取決於微波爐和目標溫度。","2–4 minutes is the water-bath time for a small tokkuri. 5 seconds is too short and 10 minutes far too long.":"2–4 分鐘是小德利隔水加熱的時間。5 秒太短，10 分鐘太長。","What is a tokkuri?":"德利（tokkuri）是什麼？","The carafe for heating and serving sake":"用來加熱和倒清酒的酒壺","The cedar box":"杉木盒","The kōji room":"麴室","The tokkuri is the carafe, usually pottery, holding 150–300 mL.":"德利是酒壺，通常是陶製，容量 150–300 mL。","The cup is the o-choko and the cedar box is the masu.":"酒杯是豬口，杉木盒是木升。","Why does an o-choko need frequent refilling?":"為什麼豬口（o-choko）需要經常斟酒？","It is small: frequent refilling reflects Japanese hospitality":"它很小：經常斟酒反映了日本的待客之道","It leaks from the base":"底部會漏","The wood absorbs the sake":"木頭會吸收清酒","The sake evaporates at once":"清酒會立刻蒸發","Small cups empty quickly: refilling them is a gesture of hospitality.":"小杯子很快就空了：為人斟酒是一種待客的表現。","The most common o-choko are pottery or glass, not wood; wood is the masu.":"最常見的豬口是陶製或玻璃製，不是木製；木製的是木升。","Why is a masu not an ideal vessel for sake?":"為什麼木升（masu）不是理想的清酒器具？","The cedar wood adds flavour to the sake":"杉木會為清酒增添風味","It is too large":"它太大了","It is made of fragile glass":"它是易碎的玻璃做的","It overheats the sake":"它會讓清酒過熱","The masu is cedar, and the wood changes the sake's flavour.":"木升是杉木做的，木頭會改變清酒的風味。","The masu is a small wooden box, not glass; it was once used to measure rice and now appears at some ceremonies.":"木升是小木盒，不是玻璃；它以前用來量米，現在出現在一些儀式中。","Which glass is ideal for a chilled daiginjō?":"冰涼的大吟釀最適合用哪種杯子？","A small tulip-shaped wine glass":"小的鬱金香形葡萄酒杯","A large beer mug":"大啤酒杯","A wide tumbler":"寬口平底杯","Small to keep the temperature, tulip-shaped to enhance the aromas.":"小杯能保持溫度，鬱金香形能突顯香氣。","A big mug lets the sake warm up, a masu adds wood flavour, and a wide tumbler lets aromas escape.":"大啤酒杯會讓清酒變溫，木升會增加木頭味，寬口平底杯會讓香氣散失。","In a pairing, which affects which more?":"在餐酒搭配中，哪一方對另一方的影響較大？","Food affects the drink more than the reverse":"食物對飲料的影響大於反過來","The drink affects the food more":"飲料對食物的影響較大","They never affect each other":"兩者從不互相影響","It depends only on colour":"只取決於顏色","Generally food changes how the drink tastes more than the reverse.":"一般來說，食物改變飲料的味道，多於飲料改變食物。","Interactions always exist, even if usually mild; colour has nothing to do with it.":"交互作用一定存在，即使通常不明顯；顏色與此無關。","What effect do salt and acidity in food have on a drink?":"食物中的鹹味和酸味對飲料有什麼影響？","Less bitter and acidic, sweeter and fruitier":"苦味和酸度降低，更甜、更有果香","More bitter and acidic, less fruity":"苦味和酸度增加，果香減少","More alcohol burn":"酒精灼熱感增加","Salt and acidity are positive interactions: they soften the drink.":"鹹味和酸味是正面的交互作用：會讓飲料變得柔和。","More bitter and acidic is the effect of umami, sugar and chilli; alcohol burn rises with chilli.":"苦味和酸度增加是鮮味、甜味和辣椒的影響；酒精灼熱感會因辣椒而增加。","Which element in food makes a drink more bitter, more acidic and less fruity?":"食物中的哪個元素會讓飲料更苦、更酸、果香更少？","Salt":"鹽","Fat":"油脂","Umami is the main 'difficult' element in sake pairings.":"鮮味是清酒搭配中主要的「難搭」元素。","Salt and acidity have the opposite effect, and salt can even cancel out umami.":"鹹味和酸味的效果相反，鹽甚至可以抵銷鮮味。","A very spicy (chilli-hot) dish with a sake: what happens?":"非常辣（辣椒辣）的菜配清酒：會發生什麼？","The alcohol burn increases":"酒精灼熱感增加","The sake seems sweeter":"清酒喝起來更甜","The sake seems less acidic":"清酒喝起來酸度更低","The heat disappears":"辣味消失","Chilli acts like umami and also increases the burning effect of the alcohol.":"辣椒的作用和鮮味類似，還會增加酒精的灼熱感。","Chilli makes the drink less sweet and more acidic, not the reverse, and it does not vanish.":"辣椒讓飲料更不甜、更酸，不是相反，而且辣味不會消失。","What is the most important consideration when pairing sake with food?":"清酒配餐時最重要的考量是什麼？","Personal preference":"個人喜好","Only using Japanese food":"只搭配日本料理","Always serving sake warm":"清酒一定要溫熱","Choosing the most expensive sake":"選最貴的清酒","Most sakes go with most foods, so personal preference decides.":"大多數清酒能搭配大多數食物，所以由個人喜好決定。","Sake also suits international food; temperature and price are not pairing criteria.":"清酒也適合各國料理；溫度和價格都不是搭配的標準。","A very sweet dessert: which sake should you choose?":"非常甜的甜點：應該選哪種清酒？","A sake at least as sweet":"至少一樣甜的清酒","The driest sake possible":"盡可能不甜的清酒","A very acidic sake":"非常酸的清酒","A very hot sake":"非常燙的清酒","Sweet dishes call for a sake at least as sweet.":"甜的菜餚需要至少一樣甜的清酒。","Sugar in the dish makes a drink seem less sweet and more acidic: a dry or acidic sake would taste thin and sour.":"菜餚中的糖會讓飲料喝起來較不甜、較酸：不甜或酸的清酒會顯得單薄又酸。","Why pair delicate sashimi with a light sake?":"為什麼細緻的生魚片要搭配清淡的清酒？","So food and sake have a similar flavour intensity":"讓食物和清酒的風味強度相近","Because light sake is always more expensive":"因為清淡的清酒一定比較貴","Because fish only goes with warm sake":"因為魚只能配溫熱的清酒","Because light sake is stronger":"因為清淡的清酒比較烈","Similar intensities stop one overwhelming the other.":"強度相近能避免一方壓過另一方。","Price, temperature and strength are not the reason for this advice.":"價格、溫度和酒精濃度都不是這個建議的理由。","A glass of rice":"一杯米釀的酒","Sake is a Japanese alcoholic drink made from rice. In Japan one of its official names is nihon-shu, which simply means 'the alcoholic drink of Japan': the name alone tells you how closely sake is tied to its homeland. First-time tasters often expect something fiery, like a spirit, and discover instead a soft, fresh and delicate drink.":"清酒是以米釀造的日本酒精飲料。在日本，它的正式名稱之一是「日本酒」（nihon-shu），意思就是「日本的酒精飲料」：光是這個名稱就說明了清酒與故鄉的緊密關係。第一次品嘗的人常以為會像烈酒一樣嗆辣，結果卻發現它柔和、新鮮又細緻。","Most sakes are clear and colourless; a few show a faint pale yellow or green tint. A typical sake has 15–17% abv, a little more than most wines. On the palate it is usually slightly sweet and lightly acidic, with gentle flavours that recall cereal, dairy (think yoghurt or cream) or fruit.":"大多數清酒清澈無色；少數帶有淡淡的淺黃或淺綠色調。典型清酒的酒精濃度為 15–17% abv，比大多數葡萄酒略高。入口通常微甜、酸度輕，風味柔和，讓人想到穀物、乳製品（像優格或鮮奶油）或水果。","Beware a common mix-up: sake is not a spirit. It is made by fermentation, like wine and beer. Keeping the 'typical' profile in mind (clear, 15–17%, slightly sweet, lightly acidic, delicate flavours) makes every later style easier to understand, because many styles are defined by how they differ from this starting point.":"小心一個常見的混淆：清酒不是烈酒。它和葡萄酒、啤酒一樣是發酵製成的。記住「典型」的樣貌（清澈、15–17%、微甜、酸度輕、風味細緻），之後的每種風格都會更容易理解，因為許多風格就是以它們和這個起點的差異來定義的。","Sake: a Japanese alcoholic drink made from rice; official name nihon-shu":"清酒：以米釀造的日本酒精飲料；正式名稱「日本酒」","Usually clear and colourless, sometimes pale yellow or green":"通常清澈無色，有時帶淺黃或淺綠色","Typically 15–17% abv, slightly sweet, lightly acidic":"通常 15–17% abv，微甜、酸度輕","Delicate cereal, dairy or fruity flavours; fermented, not distilled":"細緻的穀物、乳製品或果香風味；發酵而成，不是蒸餾","Four ingredients, plus one optional extra":"四種原料，加上一種可選的添加物","Every sake starts from four main ingredients: steamed white rice, kōji, water and yeast. There is also a fifth, optional ingredient: a high-strength distilled alcohol. It will come up again and again, because whether it is used or not matters a great deal in the names of sake categories.":"每一款清酒都從四種主要原料開始：蒸過的白米、麴、水和酵母。另外還有第五種可選的原料：高濃度蒸餾酒精。它會一再出現，因為是否使用它，對清酒類別的名稱影響很大。","Rice is usually bought from suppliers and reaches the brewery raw, hard and dusty: the brewer steams it so that it softens and can break up in water. Kōji, on the other hand, is made in the brewery: a small part of the steamed rice is taken and a special mould is grown on it. Without kōji there would be no way to make the alcohol found in every sake.":"米通常向供應商購買，送到酒藏時是生的、堅硬又帶粉塵：釀酒師會把它蒸過，讓它變軟，能在水中分解。麴則是在酒藏裡製作：取一小部分蒸米，在上面培養一種特殊黴菌。沒有麴，就無法產生每一款清酒中的酒精。","Water comes from local supplies: almost any water will do, as long as it is clean. Yeasts are tiny microorganisms; breweries use specific varieties, usually bought from specialist suppliers. An easy way to remember it: rice arrives from outside but is prepared in the brewery, kōji is born in the brewery, and yeast is carefully chosen.":"水來自當地水源：幾乎任何水都可以，只要乾淨。酵母是微小的微生物；酒藏使用特定的品種，通常向專門供應商購買。簡單的記法：米從外面來但在酒藏裡處理，麴在酒藏裡誕生，酵母則經過精心挑選。","Four ingredients: steamed white rice, kōji, water, yeast":"四種原料：蒸過的白米、麴、水、酵母","Optional: high-strength distilled alcohol":"可選：高濃度蒸餾酒精","Kōji: mould grown on a small part of the steamed rice; essential for any alcohol":"麴：在一小部分蒸米上培養的黴菌；產生酒精不可或缺","Local, clean water; chosen yeasts bought from specialist suppliers":"當地乾淨的水；向專門供應商購買的精選酵母","From starch to sugar, from sugar to alcohol":"從澱粉到糖，從糖到酒精","To make alcohol you need sugar dissolved in water. Yeast is added: it eats the sugar, turns it into alcohol and releases carbon dioxide, a gas. This is alcoholic fermentation, the same process behind wine and beer.":"要產生酒精，需要溶在水中的糖。加入酵母：它吃掉糖，把糖變成酒精，並釋放出二氧化碳這種氣體。這就是酒精發酵，和葡萄酒、啤酒背後的過程相同。","The catch is that rice contains no sugar: it is full of starch. Starch is made of sugar molecules bonded together in long chains, and yeast cannot eat it. You need substances called enzymes, which can break those bonds and free the sugars one by one. This step is called starch conversion.":"問題在於米不含糖：米裡滿是澱粉。澱粉由糖分子連結成的長鏈構成，酵母無法吃它。需要一種叫做酵素的物質，能切斷這些鍵結，把糖一個個釋放出來。這個步驟稱為澱粉轉化。","This is why kōji matters so much: the mould growing on the rice produces exactly these enzymes. When steamed rice, kōji and water are mixed, the enzymes turn starch into sugar and the yeast turns the sugar into alcohol. No kōji, no sugar; no sugar, no alcohol.":"這就是麴如此重要的原因：長在米上的黴菌正好會產生這些酵素。蒸米、麴和水混合後，酵素把澱粉變成糖，酵母再把糖變成酒精。沒有麴就沒有糖；沒有糖就沒有酒精。","Alcoholic fermentation: sugar + yeast → alcohol + carbon dioxide":"酒精發酵：糖 + 酵母 → 酒精 + 二氧化碳","Rice contains starch, not sugar; yeast cannot eat starch":"米含有澱粉而不是糖；酵母不能吃澱粉","Starch conversion: enzymes break starch into sugars":"澱粉轉化：酵素把澱粉分解成糖","The enzymes come from the kōji mould":"酵素來自麴菌","Three steps that always happen":"一定會發生的三個步驟","Once the steamed rice, kōji, water and yeast are ready, brewing proper can begin. Three steps always happen: fermentation, filtration and bottling. Three more are optional: adding alcohol, adding water and pasteurisation. It pays to learn the two lists separately.":"蒸米、麴、水和酵母都準備好之後，真正的釀造就可以開始了。有三個步驟一定會發生：發酵、過濾和裝瓶。另外三個是可選的：添加酒精、加水和加熱殺菌。把這兩份清單分開記會很有幫助。","Fermentation starts small: a little of the ingredients is used to make a starter that builds a healthy, active yeast population. Little by little, more rice, kōji and water are then blended into the starter until the batch is much bigger: the main fermentation. While it ferments, the liquid is white and cloudy, coloured by the rice.":"發酵從小規模開始：用少量原料做成酒母，培養出健康、活躍的酵母群。接著一點一點地把更多米、麴和水加入酒母，直到批量大很多：這就是主發酵。發酵時液體是白色混濁的，顏色來自米。","When fermentation is over, the law requires every sake to be filtered: the solid rice residue is removed, usually leaving a clear liquid. Then comes bottling: nearly all sake is packed in glass bottles of various sizes.":"發酵結束後，法律規定每一款清酒都必須過濾：去除米的固形殘渣，通常留下清澈的液體。接著是裝瓶：幾乎所有清酒都裝在各種容量的玻璃瓶裡。","Always: fermentation, filtration, bottling":"一定會有：發酵、過濾、裝瓶","Optional: adding alcohol, adding water, pasteurisation":"可選：添加酒精、加水、加熱殺菌","Fermentation starter → main fermentation":"酒母 → 主發酵","Filtration is required by law; nearly all sake goes into glass bottles":"過濾是法律規定的；幾乎所有清酒都裝進玻璃瓶","Parallel fermentation":"並行發酵","Something special happens in sake: while the kōji enzymes are turning starch into sugar, the yeast is already eating that sugar and making alcohol. Both reactions take place at the same time, in the same vessel, from the start to the end of fermentation. This is why it is called 'parallel fermentation', a feature unique to sake brewing.":"清酒有個特別之處：麴的酵素把澱粉變成糖的同時，酵母已經在吃這些糖並產生酒精。兩個反應在同一個容器中同時進行，從發酵開始一直到結束。這就是它被稱為「並行發酵」的原因，是清酒釀造獨有的特點。","Picture a kitchen where one cook slices bread while another eats each slice straight away: there is never much bread piling up on the board. In the same way, sugar is made and used up little by little in the tank. The result is a liquid that usually reaches about 20% abv by the end of fermentation, more than sake normally has in the bottle.":"想像一個廚房，一位廚師切麵包，另一位馬上把每一片吃掉：砧板上從來不會堆積太多麵包。同樣地，槽裡的糖也是一點一點地產生又被用掉。結果是發酵結束時液體通常達到約 20% abv，比瓶裝清酒一般的濃度還高。","Note the contrast with beer, where starch is turned into sugar first and fermented afterwards, in two separate stages. In sake the two overlap: it is exactly this overlap that earns the name parallel fermentation.":"注意與啤酒的差別：啤酒是先把澱粉變成糖，之後再發酵，分成兩個獨立的階段。清酒的兩個階段則重疊在一起：正是這種重疊讓它得到「並行發酵」的名稱。","Parallel fermentation: sugar is made and eaten at the same time":"並行發酵：糖同時被產生和被吃掉","It is unique to sake brewing":"這是清酒釀造獨有的","At the end of fermentation: about 20% abv":"發酵結束時：約 20% abv","Three optional choices: alcohol, water, heat":"三個可選擇項：酒精、水、加熱","Before filtration the brewer may add a high-strength distilled alcohol with a neutral flavour. Its purpose is not to make the sake stronger: it helps to pull out flavours trapped in the solid rice residue. Sakes made without this addition carry a special word on the label, which we will meet soon: junmai.":"過濾前，釀酒師可以添加風味中性的高濃度蒸餾酒精。目的不是讓清酒更烈：而是幫助萃取困在米固形殘渣中的風味。沒有這項添加的清酒，酒標上會有一個特別的詞，我們很快就會認識它：純米。","Water is nearly always added before bottling. Fermentation leaves the sake at around 20% abv; water brings it down to the usual 15–17%. That is why bottled sake is a little lower in alcohol than the freshly fermented liquid.":"裝瓶前幾乎都會加水。發酵後的清酒約為 20% abv；加水把它降到一般的 15–17%。這就是為什麼瓶裝清酒的酒精濃度比剛發酵完的液體略低。","Finally, the vast majority of sake is heat-treated before it goes on sale: this is pasteurisation. It prevents faulty aromas from developing and makes the sake more stable. Sakes that skip this step form a special style, nama, which needs far more careful handling.":"最後，絕大多數清酒在上市前都會經過熱處理：這就是加熱殺菌（火入）。它能防止缺陷香氣產生，讓清酒更穩定。省略這一步的清酒構成一種特殊風格：生酒，需要更小心地處理。","Neutral alcohol added before filtration: extracts flavours from the rice solids":"過濾前添加中性酒精：從米固形物中萃取風味","Water nearly always added before bottling: from about 20% down to 15–17%":"裝瓶前幾乎都會加水：從約 20% 降到 15–17%","Pasteurisation for the vast majority: prevents faulty aromas":"絕大多數會加熱殺菌：防止缺陷香氣","Polishing: removing rice to choose a style":"精米：去除米粒來選擇風格","The raw material is brown rice. Four stages turn it into the steamed white rice a brewery needs: polishing, washing, soaking and steaming. The first matters most for style. During polishing the grains pass between two rough surfaces that, over a few hours, slowly wear away the outer layers and some of the middle layers.":"原料是糙米。四個階段把它變成酒藏需要的蒸白米：精米、洗米、浸泡和蒸米。第一個階段對風格最重要。精米時，米粒在兩個粗糙的表面之間通過，在數小時內慢慢磨掉外層和部分中層。","A rice grain has three zones. The outer layers are brown; the middle layers are white; at the centre is a white core that is almost pure starch. The outer and middle layers also hold starch, but together with many other components: these give sake more acidity and umami, with cereal and lactic aromas. The outer layers are nearly always removed; the real decision concerns the middle layers.":"米粒有三個區域。外層是褐色的；中層是白色的；中心是幾乎純澱粉的白色米芯。外層和中層也含有澱粉，但還有許多其他成分：這些成分讓清酒有更多酸度和鮮味，以及穀物和乳酸香氣。外層幾乎都會被去除；真正要決定的是中層。","When only part of the middle layers is polished away, the result is a sake with higher acidity and umami and aromas on the cereal and lactic side. If most or all of the middle layers go, leaving mainly the starch core, the sake will usually have less acidity and umami and more fruity, floral aromas. Polishing more means moving towards a purer, more fragrant style.":"只磨掉部分中層時，得到的清酒酸度和鮮味較高，香氣偏向穀物和乳酸。如果磨掉大部分或全部中層，主要留下澱粉米芯，清酒通常酸度和鮮味較少，果香和花香較多。精米越多，就越走向純淨、芳香的風格。","Four stages: polishing, washing, soaking, steaming":"四個階段：精米、洗米、浸泡、蒸米","Outer layers brown, middle layers white, core almost pure starch":"外層褐色，中層白色，米芯幾乎是純澱粉","Light polishing → more acidity and umami, cereal and lactic aromas":"輕度精米 → 更多酸度和鮮味，穀物和乳酸香氣","Heavy polishing → less acidity and umami, more fruit and flowers":"高度精米 → 酸度和鮮味較少，更多果香和花香","The polishing ratio: a number that works backwards":"精米步合：一個反著看的數字","How much rice must be removed to qualify for a premium grade is set by Japanese law: this is the polishing ratio. Thanks to this rule, the label terms give a reliable guide to the style of the sake.":"要符合高級等級必須去除多少米，是由日本法律規定的：這就是精米步合。因為有這項規定，酒標用語能可靠地反映清酒的風格。","The figure is a percentage and shows how much of the grain REMAINS, not how much is removed. If the law requires 60%, then 60% or less of the original grain must be left after polishing: at least 40% has gone. The lower the number, the more the rice has been polished.":"這個數字是百分比，表示米粒「留下」多少，而不是去除多少。如果法律要求 60%，精米後必須只留下原本米粒的 60% 以下：至少去除了 40%。數字越低，米精得越多。","Three thresholds to remember. 70% or less: honjōzō and most junmai. 60% or less: ginjō and junmai ginjō. 50% or less: daiginjō and junmai daiginjō. Junmai may in theory use rice with a ratio between 100% and 70%, but this is very rare.":"要記住三個門檻。70% 以下：本釀造和大多數純米。60% 以下：吟釀和純米吟釀。50% 以下：大吟釀和純米大吟釀。理論上純米可以使用精米步合在 100% 到 70% 之間的米，但非常少見。","Polishing ratio: % of the grain that remains, set by law":"精米步合：米粒留下的百分比，由法律規定","Lower number = more polished rice":"數字越低 = 米精得越多","≤70% honjōzō and most junmai; ≤60% ginjō and junmai ginjō; ≤50% daiginjō and junmai daiginjō":"≤70% 本釀造和大多數純米；≤60% 吟釀和純米吟釀；≤50% 大吟釀和純米大吟釀","Junmai between 100% and 70%: allowed but very rare":"純米在 100% 到 70% 之間：允許但非常少見","Washing, soaking and steaming":"洗米、浸泡和蒸米","After polishing, the grains are covered in rice dust: exactly the outer and middle layers the brewer wanted to get rid of. That is why the rice must be washed carefully; otherwise the dust would put back into the sake what has just been removed.":"精米後，米粒上沾滿米粉：正是釀酒師想去除的外層和中層。所以米必須仔細清洗；否則這些粉末會把剛去除的東西又帶回清酒裡。","Next come soaking and steaming. Together they soften the grain and bring it to the texture and moisture level it needs to fall apart in the liquid once fermentation is under way. Rice that is too hard would not break down; rice that is too wet would turn into a mush that is hard to work with.":"接著是浸泡和蒸米。兩者一起讓米粒變軟，讓它達到適當的質地和含水量，在發酵開始後能在液體中散開。太硬的米不會分解；太濕的米則會變成難以處理的糊狀。","Washing: removes the rice dust left by polishing":"洗米：去除精米留下的米粉","Soaking and steaming: the right texture and moisture":"浸泡和蒸米：適當的質地和水分","Goal: a grain that breaks up in the water during fermentation":"目標：讓米粒在發酵中能在水裡散開","Kōji is made in a special series of rooms in the brewery, where the brewer can control temperature and humidity very precisely. This matters: the mould is alive, and it grows faster or slower depending on the warmth and moisture it finds. The rooms therefore have different zones, from the warmest and most humid to the coolest and driest.":"麴是在酒藏裡一系列特別的房間中製作的，釀酒師可以在那裡非常精確地控制溫度和濕度。這很重要：黴菌是活的，會依照環境的溫度和濕度長得快或慢。因此麴室有不同的區域，從最溫暖、最潮濕到最涼、最乾燥。","The work follows four stages: cool the steamed rice, spread the mould spores, let the mould grow quickly at first, then control and finally stop its growth. In practice the rice 'travels' through the room: it goes into the warmest zone to get the mould started and moves towards the coolest zone when the growth must slow down.":"工作分為四個階段：讓蒸米冷卻、撒上黴菌孢子、讓黴菌一開始快速生長，然後控制並最終停止它的生長。實際上米會在麴室裡「移動」：先進入最溫暖的區域讓黴菌開始生長，需要減緩時再移往最涼的區域。","Dedicated rooms with precisely controlled temperature and humidity":"專用的房間，精確控制溫度和濕度","Four stages: cool, spread spores, initial growth, control and stop":"四個階段：冷卻、撒孢子、初期生長、控制與停止","Warm and humid = fast growth; cool and dry = slow growth":"溫暖潮濕 = 生長快；涼爽乾燥 = 生長慢","The four kōji stages, one by one":"製麴的四個階段，逐一說明","1. Cooling. Fresh from the steamer, the rice is too hot for kōji making: it is spread on mats to cool naturally. 2. Spreading the mould. Once cool enough, the rice moves into the warmest, most humid part of the room, is spread in a thin layer on a large table, and the mould spores (its 'seeds') are scattered over it.":"1. 冷卻。剛出蒸籠的米對製麴來說太燙：把它攤在蓆子上自然冷卻。2. 撒上黴菌。夠涼之後，米被移到麴室最溫暖、最潮濕的區域，在大桌上攤成薄薄一層，再把黴菌孢子（它的「種子」）撒在上面。","3. Initial growth. In the warm, humid zone the mould spreads quickly over the rice, which is turned regularly by hand so that the growth is even. 4. Controlling and stopping. The rice is sometimes moved onto racks in cooler, less humid zones to slow the mould and get exactly the right amount; to stop it altogether, the rice ends up in the coolest corner of the room.":"3. 初期生長。在溫暖潮濕的區域，黴菌很快就在米上擴散，並定期用手翻動米，讓生長均勻。4. 控制與停止。有時會把米移到較涼、較不潮濕區域的架子上，減緩黴菌生長，得到剛好適量的黴菌；要完全停止時，米最後會被放到麴室最涼的角落。","Natural cooling on mats":"在蓆子上自然冷卻","Spores scattered on a thin layer, in the warmest, most humid zone":"在最溫暖潮濕的區域，把孢子撒在薄薄一層米上","Fast growth, rice turned by hand":"快速生長，用手翻動米","Slow it in cooler zones, stop it in the coolest zone":"在較涼的區域減緩，在最涼的區域停止","Yeast, the real flavour maker":"酵母，真正的風味製造者","In many sake styles the steamed rice, kōji and water add relatively little flavour. In nearly all styles it is the yeast that contributes a large share of the aromas. That is why fermentation is when the brewer decides much of the sake's character.":"在許多清酒風格中，蒸米、麴和水帶來的風味相對較少。在幾乎所有風格中，大部分的香氣都來自酵母。所以釀酒師是在發酵階段決定清酒的大部分個性。","As we saw, it all begins with a small starter: a little of the four main ingredients is used to grow a healthy yeast population. The starter then moves to a larger vessel and is carefully mixed with more steamed rice, kōji and water: the main fermentation. Now the brewer has two levers: the choice of yeast and the temperature.":"如前所述，一切從小小的酒母開始：用少量四種主要原料培養出健康的酵母群。接著酒母移到較大的容器中，小心地與更多蒸米、麴和水混合：這就是主發酵。這時釀酒師有兩個手段：酵母的選擇和溫度。","Rice, kōji and water give little flavour in many styles; yeast gives a lot":"在許多風格中，米、麴和水帶來的風味很少；酵母帶來很多","Starter → main fermentation in a larger vessel":"酒母 → 在較大容器中進行主發酵","Two style levers: choice of yeast and temperature":"兩個風格手段：酵母的選擇和溫度","Cool and fragrant, or warm and cereal-like":"低溫芳香，或溫暖帶穀物感","To get the fruity, floral aromas typical of ginjō styles, two things are needed together: special yeast strains and lower fermentation temperatures. A cool fermentation is slower and keeps delicate scents such as apple, pear or white flowers.":"要得到吟釀風格典型的果香和花香，需要兩件事同時具備：特殊酵母菌株和較低的發酵溫度。低溫發酵較慢，能保留蘋果、西洋梨或白花等細緻的香氣。","Warmer fermentation temperatures, by contrast, produce cereal and lactic aromas. Link this to polishing and the picture becomes clear: highly polished rice + special yeasts + cool fermentation give fruity, floral sakes; lightly polished rice + warmer fermentation give cereal, lactic sakes with more acidity and umami.":"相反地，較高的發酵溫度會產生穀物和乳酸香氣。把這和精米連在一起看就很清楚了：高度精米 + 特殊酵母 + 低溫發酵，得到果香、花香的清酒；輕度精米 + 較溫暖的發酵，得到穀物、乳酸風格，酸度和鮮味較多的清酒。","Ginjō aromas (fruit, flowers): special yeasts + cool fermentation":"吟釀香氣（水果、花）：特殊酵母 + 低溫發酵","Warmer fermentation: cereal and lactic aromas":"較溫暖的發酵：穀物和乳酸香氣","Style = polishing + yeast + temperature":"風格 = 精米 + 酵母 + 溫度","Futsū-shu, the everyday sake":"普通酒，日常的清酒","Sake is divided into categories and grades. The main split is between basic and premium sake; premium sake is then divided into several grades. The simple, widespread basic sake is called futsū-shu in Japanese.":"清酒分成類別和等級。主要的劃分是普通清酒和高級清酒；高級清酒再分成幾個等級。簡單、普及的基本清酒在日文中稱為普通酒（futsū-shu）。","Futsū-shu fits the portrait of typical sake exactly: mostly clear and colourless (now and then pale yellow or green), 15–17% abv, a touch of sweetness, gentle acidity and discreet lactic and fruity notes. It is the reference point for understanding the premium grades.":"普通酒完全符合典型清酒的樣貌：大多清澈無色（偶爾帶淺黃或淺綠色）、15–17% abv、帶點甜味、酸度溫和，以及低調的乳酸和果香調性。它是理解高級等級的參考點。","Main division: basic and premium sake":"主要劃分：普通清酒和高級清酒","Basic sake = futsū-shu":"基本清酒 = 普通酒","Futsū-shu: 15–17%, slightly sweet, lightly acidic, delicate lactic and fruity notes":"普通酒：15–17%，微甜、酸度輕，細緻的乳酸和果香調性","Six premium grades, two style families":"六個高級等級，兩個風格家族","Among the premium grades, six matter most. Nearly all have the same strength and colour as basic sake, but they fall into two very different styles. The first covers sakes with pure floral and fruity aromas: the key word is ginjō. Daiginjō literally means 'big ginjō' and marks the sakes with the purest fruity, floral flavours.":"在高級等級中，最重要的有六個。幾乎都和普通清酒有相同的酒精濃度和顏色，但分成兩種截然不同的風格。第一種是帶有純淨花香和果香的清酒：關鍵詞是吟釀。大吟釀字面上的意思是「大的吟釀」，代表果香、花香風味最純淨的清酒。","The second style smells of cereal and dairy rather than fruit, and usually shows higher acidity and umami than any ginjō. Junmai and honjōzō belong here. In short: ginjō, junmai ginjō, daiginjō and junmai daiginjō sit on the fruit-and-flower side; junmai and honjōzō on the side of cereal, lactic notes, acidity and umami.":"第二種風格聞起來是穀物和乳製品而不是水果，通常酸度和鮮味比任何吟釀都高。純米和本釀造屬於這一類。簡單說：吟釀、純米吟釀、大吟釀和純米大吟釀在水果和花的一邊；純米和本釀造在穀物、乳酸調性、酸度和鮮味的一邊。","Six main premium grades, mostly with the strength and colour of basic sake":"六個主要高級等級，大多與普通清酒有相同的酒精濃度和顏色","Ginjō style: pure fruity, floral aromas, less acidity and umami":"吟釀風格：純淨的果香、花香，酸度和鮮味較少","Daiginjō = 'big ginjō': the purest aromas":"大吟釀 =「大的吟釀」：香氣最純淨","Junmai and honjōzō: cereal and lactic, more acidity and umami":"純米和本釀造：穀物和乳酸，酸度和鮮味較多","Junmai: pure rice, and the full grid":"純米：純粹的米，以及完整的對照表","There is a second way to split the six grades. Three carry the word junmai, meaning 'pure rice': it shows that NO distilled alcohol was added before filtration. So junmai ginjō is a style similar to ginjō; the only difference is that junmai ginjō has had no alcohol added. The same goes for daiginjō and junmai daiginjō, and for honjōzō (with alcohol) and junmai (without).":"六個等級還有第二種分法。其中三個帶有「純米」這個詞，意思是「純粹的米」：表示過濾前「沒有」添加蒸餾酒精。所以純米吟釀的風格和吟釀相似；唯一的差別是純米吟釀沒有添加酒精。大吟釀和純米大吟釀、本釀造（有酒精）和純米（沒有）也是一樣。","Now put it all in a two-column grid. Left, no added alcohol: junmai daiginjō (≤50%), junmai ginjō (≤60%), junmai (usually 70%). Right, with added alcohol: daiginjō (≤50%), ginjō (≤60%), honjōzō (≤70%). The top two rows, fruity and floral, come from highly polished rice, special yeasts and cool fermentation; the bottom row, cereal and lactic, from lightly polished rice and warmer fermentation.":"現在把全部放進兩欄的對照表。左邊，沒有添加酒精：純米大吟釀（≤50%）、純米吟釀（≤60%）、純米（通常 70%）。右邊，有添加酒精：大吟釀（≤50%）、吟釀（≤60%）、本釀造（≤70%）。上面兩列是果香和花香，來自高度精米、特殊酵母和低溫發酵；最下面一列是穀物和乳酸，來自輕度精米和較溫暖的發酵。","Junmai = 'pure rice': no distilled alcohol added":"純米 =「純粹的米」：沒有添加蒸餾酒精","Pairs: junmai daiginjō/daiginjō, junmai ginjō/ginjō, junmai/honjōzō":"成對：純米大吟釀／大吟釀、純米吟釀／吟釀、純米／本釀造","Same style within each pair: only the added alcohol differs":"每一對的風格相同：只差在添加的酒精","Ginjō: highly polished + special yeasts + cool; junmai/honjōzō: lightly polished + warmer":"吟釀：高度精米 + 特殊酵母 + 低溫；純米／本釀造：輕度精米 + 較溫暖","Four words, seven kanji":"四個詞，七個漢字","Sake labels are mostly written in Japanese, with only a few words in the Latin alphabet. To find your way, you only need to recognise four key terms: junmai, honjōzō, ginjō and daiginjō. Each term breaks down into sounds, and each sound matches a symbol called a kanji.":"清酒酒標大多以日文書寫，只有少數拉丁字母的字。要看懂酒標，只需要認得四個關鍵用語：純米（junmai）、本醸造（honjōzō）、吟醸（ginjō）和大吟醸（daiginjō）。每個用語都能拆成幾個音，每個音對應一個漢字。","Junmai = jun 純 + mai 米. Honjōzō = hon 本 + jō 醸 + zō 造. Ginjō = gin 吟 + jō 醸. Daiginjō = dai 大 + gin 吟 + jō 醸. In total there are only seven kanji to learn, because gin 吟 and jō 醸 repeat. A memory trick: 大 (dai, big) looks like a person with arms spread wide, 'big' like daiginjō, the 'big ginjō'.":"junmai = jun 純 + mai 米。honjōzō = hon 本 + jō 醸 + zō 造。ginjō = gin 吟 + jō 醸。daiginjō = dai 大 + gin 吟 + jō 醸。總共只要學七個漢字，因為吟和醸會重複出現。記憶小技巧：大（dai，大的）看起來像一個張開雙臂的人，「大」就像大吟釀，「大的吟釀」。","Labels mostly in Japanese: recognise 4 terms":"酒標大多是日文：認得 4 個用語","Only 7 kanji: gin 吟 and jō 醸 repeat":"只有 7 個漢字：吟（gin）和醸（jō）重複出現","Nama and nigori":"生酒和濁酒","There are four important speciality styles: nama, nigori, sparkling and koshu. Nama means unpasteurised sake. Because it is less stable than other sakes, most nama should be drunk soon after release and kept refrigerated at all times. On release it can taste especially lively and fresh, but it can quickly develop spicy, malty aromas that are not to everyone's taste.":"有四種重要的特殊風格：生酒、濁酒、氣泡清酒和古酒。生酒是指未經加熱殺菌的清酒。因為它比其他清酒不穩定，大多數生酒應在上市後盡快飲用，並一直冷藏。剛上市時可能嘗起來特別活潑新鮮，但很快就可能出現辛香、麥芽味的香氣，不是每個人都喜歡。","A pronunciation note: in Japanese the 's' of nama-sake softens to a 'z', so it is usually spelled nama-zake. Nigori, on the other hand, means sake that has been roughly filtered: it is cloudy because of suspended rice particles. The style varies with how much solid matter is kept: some nigori are lightly cloudy, others thick-textured with lumps of rice.":"關於發音：在日文中，nama-sake 的「s」會變成「z」，所以通常拼作 nama-zake。濁酒（nigori）則是經過粗過濾的清酒：因為懸浮的米粒而混濁。風格隨保留的固形物多寡而不同：有些濁酒只是微濁，有些則濃稠並帶有米塊。","Nama: unpasteurised, less stable, always refrigerated, drink soon":"生酒：未經加熱殺菌、較不穩定、一直冷藏、盡快飲用","Young nama: lively and fresh; later can turn spicy and malty":"年輕的生酒：活潑新鮮；之後可能變得辛香、帶麥芽味","Usually spelled nama-zake":"通常拼作 nama-zake","Nigori: roughly filtered, cloudy from rice particles":"濁酒：粗過濾，因米粒而混濁","Sparkling sake and koshu":"氣泡清酒和古酒","As with any sparkling drink, the bubbles in sparkling sake come from carbon dioxide dissolved in the liquid. There are various techniques for getting it into bottled sake. Some sparkling sakes are very light in texture and low in alcohol; others are richer-textured and more complex, and can even be nigori too. Japanese terms for these sakes exist but are little used outside Japan.":"和任何氣泡飲料一樣，氣泡清酒的氣泡來自溶在液體中的二氧化碳。把它溶進瓶裝清酒的技術有好幾種。有些氣泡清酒口感非常清淡、酒精低；有些口感較濃郁、較複雜，甚至也可以是濁酒。這些清酒有日文名稱，但在日本以外很少使用。","Koshu is the name for aged sake. It can be aged in various vessels: storage tanks, terracotta, glass bottles, wood (though oak is very rarely used). Some brewers age at room temperature, others at low temperatures, sometimes below freezing: hence a wide range of styles. Most koshu is amber or brown, richly textured and sweet, with pronounced aromas of nuts, dried fruit and even meat and pickled vegetables; but some aged sakes are paler and more delicate.":"古酒是熟成清酒的名稱。它可以在各種容器中熟成：儲存槽、陶甕、玻璃瓶、木材（但很少用橡木）。有些釀酒師在常溫下熟成，有些在低溫，有時甚至在冰點以下：因此風格非常多樣。大多數古酒呈琥珀色或褐色，口感濃郁、甜，帶有明顯的堅果、果乾，甚至肉和醃菜的香氣；但也有些熟成清酒顏色較淡、較細緻。","Sparkling: bubbles of dissolved carbon dioxide; from light and low-alcohol to rich and complex":"氣泡清酒：溶解的二氧化碳形成氣泡；從清淡低酒精到濃郁複雜","Koshu = aged sake; various vessels, oak is rare":"古酒 = 熟成清酒；各種容器，很少用橡木","Typical koshu: amber/brown, rich, sweet, nuts, dried fruit, even meat and pickles":"典型古酒：琥珀色／褐色、濃郁、甜、堅果、果乾，甚至肉和醃菜","Speciality styles and grades combine":"特殊風格和等級可以結合","A point that confuses many: speciality styles are not a separate category from the grades. A nama, nigori, sparkling sake or koshu can be a futsū-shu or any of the premium grades. Take a junmai ginjō: nothing stops it from being nama, nigori or sparkling as well, or several of these at once.":"很多人會混淆的一點：特殊風格不是和等級分開的類別。生酒、濁酒、氣泡清酒或古酒，可以是普通酒，也可以是任何一個高級等級。以純米吟釀為例：沒有什麼能阻止它同時也是生酒、濁酒或氣泡清酒，甚至同時是其中好幾種。","Think of the grade as 'how the sake was made' (polishing, alcohol added or not) and the speciality style as 'one extra treatment': no pasteurisation, rough filtration, bubbles, ageing. Both pieces of information can therefore appear on one label, for example a 'junmai nigori nama'.":"把等級想成「清酒是怎麼做的」（精米、有沒有添加酒精），把特殊風格想成「一項額外的處理」：不加熱殺菌、粗過濾、氣泡、熟成。所以兩種資訊可以出現在同一張酒標上，例如「純米 濁酒 生酒」（junmai nigori nama）。","Speciality styles apply to futsū-shu and to every premium grade":"特殊風格適用於普通酒和所有高級等級","One sake can combine several speciality styles":"一款清酒可以結合多種特殊風格","Grade = how it is made; speciality = one extra treatment":"等級 = 怎麼做的；特殊風格 = 一項額外處理","Getting ready to taste":"準備品飲","To get the most from a tasting you need the same method for every sample: that way your notes describe each sake accurately and are still useful when you reread them later. Before pouring, prepare the setting: no distracting smells (perfume, cooking, smoke) and a white surface for judging the sake's appearance.":"要充分發揮品飲的效果，每個樣品都要用相同的方法：這樣你的筆記才能準確描述每一款清酒，日後重讀時也仍然有用。倒酒之前先準備環境：沒有干擾的氣味（香水、烹飪、煙），並準備白色表面來判斷清酒的外觀。","The right glass has a wide bowl that narrows towards the top: it concentrates the aromas and makes the nose easier to assess. Finally, your palate should be clean, with no lingering flavours such as toothpaste or coffee, which would distort everything.":"合適的杯子杯身寬、向杯口收窄：能集中香氣，讓聞香更容易評估。最後，口腔要乾淨，沒有牙膏或咖啡等殘留味道，否則會干擾一切。","Same method for every sample":"每個樣品用相同的方法","No distracting smells; a white surface":"沒有干擾的氣味；白色表面","Glass with a wide bowl narrowing at the top":"杯身寬、向杯口收窄的杯子","Clean palate: no toothpaste or coffee":"口腔乾淨：沒有牙膏或咖啡","A simple grid: appearance, nose, palate":"簡單的表格：外觀、香氣、口感","Appearance: check clarity, from clear to cloudy (a nigori will be cloudy), and note any bubbles and the colour. Nose: look for the aroma families (fruity/floral, cereal/grain/nut, lactic/dairy, other) and note intensity and any faults.":"外觀：檢查清澈度，從清澈到混濁（濁酒會是混濁的），並記下是否有氣泡及顏色。香氣：找出香氣類別（果香／花香、穀物／穀粒／堅果、乳酸／乳製品、其他），並記下強度和任何缺陷。","Palate: judge flavour intensity (light, medium, pronounced) and the same families as on the nose; then everything else: sweetness, acidity, texture, umami, any rice particles, bubbles and the finish. With this grid a ginjō (fruity, delicate) and a junmai (cereal, more acidity and umami) stand apart at once.":"口感：判斷風味強度（淡、中等、明顯），以及和香氣相同的類別；接著是其他一切：甜度、酸度、口感質地、鮮味、是否有米粒、氣泡和餘韻。用這張表，吟釀（果香、細緻）和純米（穀物、酸度和鮮味較多）一下子就能分辨出來。","Appearance: clear–cloudy, bubbles, colour":"外觀：清澈–混濁、氣泡、顏色","Nose: fruity/floral, cereal/grain/nut, lactic/dairy, other; intensity, faults":"香氣：果香／花香、穀物／穀粒／堅果、乳酸／乳製品、其他；強度、缺陷","Palate: light–medium–pronounced; sweetness, acidity, texture, umami, particles, bubbles, finish":"口感：淡–中等–明顯；甜度、酸度、質地、鮮味、米粒、氣泡、餘韻","Four rules for storing sake":"保存清酒的四個原則","Sake is easily damaged by heat and strong light. Rule one: keep it cool, even for short periods, ideally in the fridge; cold is essential for nama and for protecting the fruity aromas of ginjō. Rule two: drink it young. Most sake is meant to be drunk within about a year of going on sale; after that it loses freshness. Nama loses it within a few months.":"清酒很容易受熱和強光損害。原則一：保持涼爽，即使是短時間也一樣，最好放冰箱；低溫對生酒和保護吟釀的果香來說不可或缺。原則二：趁年輕喝。大多數清酒應在上市後約一年內喝完；之後會失去新鮮感。生酒則在幾個月內就會失去新鮮感。","Rule three: store the bottle upright. Rule four: avoid bright light, both sunshine and strong artificial light, which can cause faulty aromas. An opened bottle should be resealed and kept in the fridge: it usually lasts about two weeks, sometimes longer; the more delicate ginjō styles stay fresh for about a week.":"原則三：酒瓶直立存放。原則四：避開強光，包括陽光和強烈的人工光源，它們可能造成缺陷香氣。開過的酒瓶應重新封好放進冰箱：通常能保存約兩週，有時更久；較細緻的吟釀風格約能保持新鮮一週。","Cool, ideally refrigerated (especially nama and ginjō)":"涼爽，最好冷藏（尤其是生酒和吟釀）","Drink young: within about a year; nama within a few months":"趁年輕喝：約一年內；生酒在幾個月內","Bottle upright, away from bright light":"酒瓶直立，避開強光","Opened: in the fridge, about 2 weeks; ginjō about 1":"開瓶後：放冰箱，約 2 週；吟釀約 1 週","Recognising faults":"辨認缺陷","The most common faults come from poor storage. Oxidation: a bottle left open too long develops unwelcome caramel and toffee aromas and turns deeper in colour. Out of condition: a bottle that is too old shows caramel, toffee and pickled-vegetable aromas, again with a deeper colour. Nama-hine: nama sakes can develop unpleasant smells of malt, meat and rotting vegetables.":"最常見的缺陷來自保存不當。氧化：開瓶太久的酒會出現不討喜的焦糖和太妃糖香氣，顏色變深。狀態不佳：太老的酒會有焦糖、太妃糖和醃菜的香氣，顏色同樣變深。生老香（nama-hine）：生酒可能出現麥芽、肉和腐爛蔬菜的難聞氣味。","Interestingly, the aromas of oxidation and 'out of condition' resemble those of koshu: the chemical processes are the same. The difference is intention: in koshu they are wanted, in other sakes consumers do not want them. A word on health: in moderation alcohol is not regarded as harmful, but in excess it damages health in the short and long term; it is best to follow your country's official low-risk drinking guidelines.":"有趣的是，氧化和「狀態不佳」的香氣與古酒的香氣相似：化學過程是一樣的。差別在於意圖：在古酒中是刻意追求的，在其他清酒中則是消費者不想要的。關於健康：適量飲酒不被視為有害，但過量飲酒會在短期和長期損害健康；最好遵循你所在國家官方的低風險飲酒指引。","Oxidation (open too long): caramel, toffee, deeper colour":"氧化（開瓶太久）：焦糖、太妃糖、顏色變深","Out of condition (too old): caramel, toffee, pickled vegetables, deeper colour":"狀態不佳（太老）：焦糖、太妃糖、醃菜、顏色變深","Nama-hine (nama): malt, meat, rotting vegetables":"生老香（生酒）：麥芽、肉、腐爛蔬菜","Aromas like koshu's, but unwanted in other sakes":"香氣像古酒，但在其他清酒中是不受歡迎的","Chilled, room temperature or warm?":"冰涼、常溫還是溫熱？","Sake can be served at many temperatures, but not every style takes heat well. Three ranges to remember: chilled 6–13 °C (43–55 °F), room temperature 15–18 °C (59–64 °F), warm/hot 40–50 °C (104–122 °F). Heat changes the impression a sake gives: it comes across as more intense, fuller in texture and more acidic, which can be useful at the table. Ginjō styles, however, lose their aromas and flavours when heated: serve them chilled.":"清酒可以在許多溫度下飲用，但不是每種風格都適合加熱。要記住三個範圍：冰涼 6–13 °C（43–55 °F）、常溫 15–18 °C（59–64 °F）、溫熱／熱 40–50 °C（104–122 °F）。加熱會改變清酒給人的印象：喝起來更強烈、口感更飽滿、酸度更明顯，在餐桌上可能很有用。不過吟釀風格加熱後會失去香氣和風味：要冰涼飲用。","Summary by style: futsū-shu, junmai and honjōzō suit all three temperatures. Ginjō: chilled; room temperature is personal preference; not warm. Sparkling: chilled only. Koshu and nigori: generally best chilled, but some can be served warm. To serve chilled, the fridge is enough: pour from the refrigerated bottle or decant into a tokkuri, some of which have a compartment for iced water.":"依風格整理：普通酒、純米和本釀造適合三種溫度。吟釀：冰涼；常溫看個人喜好；不要溫熱。氣泡清酒：只能冰涼。古酒和濁酒：一般最適合冰涼，但有些可以溫熱飲用。要冰涼飲用，冰箱就夠了：直接從冷藏的酒瓶倒，或倒進德利，有些德利還有放冰水的隔層。","Chilled 6–13 °C · room 15–18 °C · warm 40–50 °C":"冰涼 6–13 °C · 常溫 15–18 °C · 溫熱 40–50 °C","Warmth makes it seem more intense, richer and more acidic":"溫熱讓它喝起來更強烈、更濃郁、酸度更明顯","Futsū-shu, junmai, honjōzō: all three":"普通酒、純米、本釀造：三種都可以","Ginjō: chilled, never warm · sparkling: chilled only · koshu, nigori: usually chilled":"吟釀：冰涼，絕不溫熱 · 氣泡清酒：只能冰涼 · 古酒、濁酒：通常冰涼","Warming sake: water bath or microwave":"溫清酒：隔水加熱或微波爐","Water bath: stand the tokkuri in a container of water heated to about 80 °C (176 °F). A small tokkuri takes two to four minutes, and the temperature should always be checked with a thermometer to stay in the right 40–50 °C range.":"隔水加熱：把德利放進裝有約 80 °C（176 °F）熱水的容器中。小德利需要二到四分鐘，而且一定要用溫度計確認溫度，保持在 40–50 °C 的正確範圍。","Microwave: use a microwave-safe container. Warming 180 mL of sake takes about 20–40 seconds, depending on the microwave and how much warmer you want it. Because water heats at almost the same rate as sake, you can practise with water first.":"微波爐：使用可微波的容器。加熱 180 mL 清酒約需 20–40 秒，取決於微波爐和想要的溫度。因為水加熱的速度和清酒幾乎一樣，可以先用水練習。","Water bath: water at about 80 °C, 2–4 minutes for a small tokkuri":"隔水加熱：約 80 °C 的水，小德利 2–4 分鐘","Always check with a thermometer":"一定要用溫度計確認","Microwave: 20–40 seconds for 180 mL, microwave-safe container":"微波爐：180 mL 需 20–40 秒，使用可微波的容器","Practise with water first":"先用水練習","Tokkuri, o-choko, masu and wine glasses":"德利、豬口、木升和葡萄酒杯","Sake can be served in various traditional Japanese vessels as well as wine glasses. The tokkuri is the sake carafe, used for heating and serving: it comes in many shapes and materials, and the most common hold about 150–300 mL and are made of pottery. The o-choko is the traditional cup: usually small, pottery or glass, with a flat base. Being small, it needs frequent refilling, reflecting a tradition of Japanese hospitality.":"清酒可以用各種日本傳統器具飲用，也可以用葡萄酒杯。德利（tokkuri）是清酒酒壺，用來加熱和倒酒：形狀和材質很多，最常見的容量約 150–300 mL，是陶製的。豬口（o-choko）是傳統酒杯：通常很小，陶製或玻璃製，平底。因為小，需要經常斟酒，反映了日本待客之道的傳統。","The masu is a small box made of cedar. It is not an ideal vessel, because the wood adds flavour to the sake; historically it was used to measure rice, and today it appears occasionally at ceremonies. Wine glasses, on the other hand, are ideal for premium sake served chilled or at room temperature: preferably small, so the sake stays at the right temperature while you drink it, and tulip-shaped to enhance the aromas.":"木升（masu）是杉木做的小盒子。它不是理想的器具，因為木頭會為清酒增添風味；過去用來量米，現在偶爾出現在儀式中。相反地，葡萄酒杯非常適合冰涼或常溫飲用的高級清酒：最好小一點，讓清酒在飲用時保持適當溫度，並選鬱金香形以突顯香氣。","Tokkuri: carafe for heating and serving, usually 150–300 mL, pottery":"德利：加熱和倒酒用的酒壺，通常 150–300 mL，陶製","O-choko: small flat-based cup; refilled often (hospitality)":"豬口：小的平底酒杯；經常斟酒（待客之道）","Masu: cedar box, not ideal (wood adds flavour); ceremonial use":"木升：杉木盒，不理想（木頭會增添風味）；儀式用","Small tulip wine glasses: ideal for premium chilled or room temperature":"小的鬱金香形葡萄酒杯：適合冰涼或常溫的高級清酒","How food changes sake":"食物如何改變清酒","When food and drink meet in the mouth, each changes how the other tastes: this is a taste interaction. An extreme example is orange juice straight after brushing your teeth: it tastes bitter and sour. Usually the interactions are much milder, but they do happen, and as a rule the food changes the drink more than the drink changes the food.":"食物和飲料在口中相遇時，彼此都會改變對方的味道：這就是味覺交互作用。一個極端的例子是刷牙後馬上喝柳橙汁：嘗起來又苦又酸。通常交互作用溫和得多，但確實會發生，而且一般來說食物改變飲料多於飲料改變食物。","Positive interactions: salt and acidity in food make a drink seem less drying, less bitter and less acidic, and also sweeter and fruitier. Negative interactions: umami in food makes a drink seem more drying, bitter and acidic, less sweet and less fruity; salt in the food can cancel this out. Sugar and chilli heat act much like umami, and chilli also increases the burn of the alcohol. Bitterness in food makes a drink taste more bitter.":"正面的交互作用：食物中的鹹味和酸味會讓飲料喝起來較不乾澀、較不苦、較不酸，也更甜、更有果香。負面的交互作用：食物中的鮮味會讓飲料喝起來更乾澀、更苦、更酸，較不甜、果香較少；食物中的鹽可以抵銷這個影響。甜味和辣椒的辣度作用很像鮮味，辣椒還會增加酒精的灼熱感。食物中的苦味會讓飲料嘗起來更苦。","Food affects a drink more than the reverse":"食物對飲料的影響大於反過來","Salt and acid: drink less bitter and acidic, sweeter and fruitier":"鹹和酸：飲料較不苦、較不酸，更甜、更有果香","Umami, sugar, chilli: more bitter and acidic, less sweet and fruity; salt offsets umami":"鮮味、甜味、辣椒：更苦、更酸，較不甜、果香較少；鹽能抵銷鮮味","Chilli: more alcohol burn; bitter food: more bitter drink":"辣椒：酒精灼熱感增加；苦的食物：飲料更苦","Three tips for pairing sake":"清酒配餐的三個建議","Sake does not only go with Japanese food: it can be enjoyed with most international dishes. It does not usually have strong negative interactions with food; when they happen, they are mostly linked to umami and sugar in the dish. So only a few key points are needed.":"清酒不只能搭配日本料理：也可以搭配大多數各國料理。它通常不會和食物產生強烈的負面交互作用；如果有，大多與菜餚中的鮮味和甜味有關。所以只需要掌握幾個重點。","First: most sakes go with most foods, so personal preference is always the most important thing. Second: food and sake should have a similar flavour intensity so that neither overwhelms the other: delicate sashimi with a light ginjō, a rich stew with a fuller junmai. Third: sweet dishes need a sake that is at least as sweet, or the sake will taste thin and sour.":"第一：大多數清酒能搭配大多數食物，所以個人喜好永遠最重要。第二：食物和清酒的風味強度應該相近，誰也不壓過誰：細緻的生魚片配清淡的吟釀，濃郁的燉菜配較飽滿的純米。第三：甜的菜餚需要至少一樣甜的清酒，否則清酒會嘗起來單薄又酸。","Sake suits international food too":"清酒也適合各國料理","Personal preference comes first":"個人喜好最優先","Match flavour intensity":"風味強度要相當","Sweet dish → sake at least as sweet":"甜的菜餚 → 至少一樣甜的清酒","Official name for sake: 'Japanese alcoholic drink'.":"清酒的正式名稱：「日本的酒精飲料」。","Typical sake: alcohol level":"典型清酒：酒精濃度","Typical sake profile":"典型清酒的樣貌","Clear, colourless, slightly sweet, lightly acidic; cereal, lactic or fruity notes.":"清澈、無色、微甜、酸度輕；穀物、乳酸或果香調性。","The four main ingredients":"四種主要原料","Steamed white rice, kōji, water, yeast.":"蒸過的白米、麴、水、酵母。","Optional ingredient":"可選原料","High-strength distilled alcohol with a neutral flavour.":"風味中性的高濃度蒸餾酒精。","Steamed rice grown with a special mould that makes enzymes.":"長了特殊黴菌、會產生酵素的蒸米。","Starch conversion":"澱粉轉化","Enzymes break starch into sugars yeast can eat.":"酵素把澱粉分解成酵母能吃的糖。","Sugar + yeast → alcohol + carbon dioxide.":"糖 + 酵母 → 酒精 + 二氧化碳。","Steps that always happen":"一定會有的步驟","Fermentation, filtration, bottling.":"發酵、過濾、裝瓶。","Optional steps":"可選的步驟","Adding alcohol, adding water, pasteurisation.":"添加酒精、加水、加熱殺菌。","Enzymes make sugar while yeast eats it, at the same time; unique to sake.":"酵素產生糖的同時酵母吃掉它；清酒獨有。","Alcohol at end of fermentation":"發酵結束時的酒精濃度","About 20% abv.":"約 20% abv。","Why add neutral alcohol?":"為什麼要添加中性酒精？","Before filtration, to extract flavours from the rice solids.":"在過濾前，從米固形物中萃取風味。","Heat treatment of most sake: prevents faulty aromas.":"大多數清酒的熱處理：防止缺陷香氣。","Brown rice to steamed rice":"從糙米到蒸米","Polishing → washing → soaking → steaming.":"精米 → 洗米 → 浸泡 → 蒸米。","The three zones of a grain":"米粒的三個區域","Brown outer, white middle, almost pure starch core.":"褐色外層、白色中層、幾乎純澱粉的米芯。","Light polishing gives…":"輕度精米帶來…","More acidity and umami, cereal and lactic aromas.":"更多酸度和鮮味，穀物和乳酸香氣。","Heavy polishing gives…":"高度精米帶來…","Less acidity and umami, fruity and floral aromas.":"酸度和鮮味較少，果香和花香。","Polishing ratio":"精米步合","Percentage of the grain that REMAINS after polishing; set by law.":"精米後米粒「留下」的百分比；由法律規定。","70% or less":"70% 以下","Honjōzō and most junmai.":"本釀造和大多數純米。","60% or less":"60% 以下","Ginjō and junmai ginjō.":"吟釀和純米吟釀。","50% or less":"50% 以下","Daiginjō and junmai daiginjō.":"大吟釀和純米大吟釀。","The four kōji stages":"製麴的四個階段","Cool, spread spores, initial growth, control and stop.":"冷卻、撒孢子、初期生長、控制與停止。","Where does the mould start?":"黴菌從哪裡開始生長？","In the warmest, most humid part of the kōji room.":"在麴室最溫暖、最潮濕的區域。","How is the mould stopped?":"如何停止黴菌生長？","By moving the rice to the coolest part of the room.":"把米移到麴室最涼的區域。","Ginjō aromas: how?":"吟釀香氣：怎麼來的？","Special yeast strains + cool fermentation.":"特殊酵母菌株 + 低溫發酵。","Warmer fermentation":"較溫暖的發酵","Cereal and lactic aromas.":"穀物和乳酸香氣。","Basic sake: 15–17%, slightly sweet, lightly acidic, lactic and fruity notes.":"基本清酒：15–17%，微甜、酸度輕，乳酸和果香調性。","Key word for the style with pure floral, fruity aromas.":"代表純淨花香、果香風格的關鍵詞。","'Big ginjō': purest fruity, floral aromas; rice ≤50%; alcohol added.":"「大的吟釀」：最純淨的果香、花香；米 ≤50%；有添加酒精。","'Pure rice': no distilled alcohol added before filtration.":"「純粹的米」：過濾前沒有添加蒸餾酒精。","Rice ≤70%, alcohol added; cereal and lactic, more acidity and umami.":"米 ≤70%，有添加酒精；穀物和乳酸，酸度和鮮味較多。","Same style and polishing (≤60%); junmai ginjō has no added alcohol.":"風格和精米相同（≤60%）；純米吟釀沒有添加酒精。","Daiginjō (dai + gin + jō); 大 = big.":"大吟釀（大 + 吟 + 釀）；大 = 大的。","Unpasteurised: always chilled, drink soon; lively, later spicy and malty.":"未經加熱殺菌：一直冷藏、盡快飲用；活潑，之後會變得辛香、帶麥芽味。","Roughly filtered: cloudy from suspended rice particles.":"粗過濾：因懸浮的米粒而混濁。","Bubbles of dissolved CO2; light to rich, sometimes nigori.":"溶解的 CO2 形成氣泡；從清淡到濃郁，有時是濁酒。","Aged sake: often amber, sweet, dried fruit, nuts; oak is rare.":"熟成清酒：常呈琥珀色、甜、果乾、堅果；很少用橡木。","Preparing a tasting":"準備品飲","No smells, white surface, glass narrowing at top, clean palate.":"沒有氣味、白色表面、杯口收窄的杯子、口腔乾淨。","Sake aroma families":"清酒的香氣類別","Fruity/floral, cereal/grain/nut, lactic/dairy, other.":"果香／花香、穀物／穀粒／堅果、乳酸／乳製品、其他。","Storing sake":"保存清酒","Cool, young (about 1 year), upright, away from bright light.":"涼爽、趁年輕（約 1 年）、直立、避開強光。","Opened bottle":"開過的酒瓶","Resealed in the fridge: about 2 weeks; ginjō about 1.":"重新封好放冰箱：約 2 週；吟釀約 1 週。","Open too long: caramel, toffee, deeper colour.":"開瓶太久：焦糖、太妃糖、顏色變深。","Too old: caramel, toffee, pickled vegetables, deeper colour.":"太老：焦糖、太妃糖、醃菜、顏色變深。","Fault in nama: malt, meat, rotting vegetables.":"生酒的缺陷：麥芽、肉、腐爛蔬菜。","Three serving temperatures":"三種侍酒溫度","Chilled 6–13 °C · room 15–18 °C · warm 40–50 °C.":"冰涼 6–13 °C · 常溫 15–18 °C · 溫熱 40–50 °C。","Ginjō: what temperature?":"吟釀：什麼溫度？","Chilled; room temperature by preference; never warm.":"冰涼；常溫看喜好；絕不溫熱。","Water bath":"隔水加熱","Water about 80 °C, 2–4 min for a small tokkuri; use a thermometer.":"約 80 °C 的水，小德利 2–4 分鐘；使用溫度計。","Microwave":"微波爐","20–40 seconds for 180 mL, in a suitable container.":"180 mL 需 20–40 秒，使用合適的容器。","Carafe for heating and serving; usually 150–300 mL, pottery.":"加熱和倒酒用的酒壺；通常 150–300 mL，陶製。","Small traditional flat-based cup; refilled often.":"傳統的平底小酒杯；經常斟酒。","Cedar box: wood adds flavour, not ideal; ceremonial use.":"杉木盒：木頭會增添風味，不理想；儀式用。","Salt and acid in food":"食物中的鹹和酸","Drink less bitter and acidic, sweeter and fruitier.":"飲料較不苦、較不酸，更甜、更有果香。","Umami in food":"食物中的鮮味","Drink more bitter, drying and acidic, less sweet and fruity.":"飲料更苦、更乾澀、更酸，較不甜、果香較少。","Three pairing rules":"三個搭配原則","Personal preference; similar intensity; sweet dish → sake at least as sweet.":"個人喜好；強度相近；甜的菜餚 → 至少一樣甜的清酒。"});(window.TWG_I18N_F=window.TWG_I18N_F||{})["zh-hant/k1"]=1;
